1
00:00:25,651 --> 00:00:27,695
(WIND BLOWING)

2
00:00:39,206 --> 00:00:41,292
(WIND CONTINUES BLOWING)

3
00:00:47,590 --> 00:00:49,675
(STORM BLOWING)

4
00:00:56,515 --> 00:00:57,767
(BREATHES HEAVILY)

5
00:01:12,406 --> 00:01:13,991
(BREATHING HEAVILY)

6
00:01:20,706 --> 00:01:22,750
(STORM CONTINUES)

7
00:01:43,062 --> 00:01:44,063
(SILENCE)

8
00:01:47,233 --> 00:01:49,151
(DISTANT CROWS CAWING)

9
00:01:52,613 --> 00:01:54,198
(SOFT HOOFBEATS)

10
00:02:19,139 --> 00:02:20,933
(BREATHING WEARILY)

11
00:02:23,352 --> 00:02:24,395
(WIND BLOWING)

12
00:02:28,607 --> 00:02:30,317
(BREATHING HEAVILY)

13
00:02:50,087 --> 00:02:51,463
BOY: Do you have any food?

14
00:02:54,133 --> 00:02:55,426
I'm very hungry.

15
00:03:09,523 --> 00:03:10,566
(SIZZLING)

16
00:03:17,865 --> 00:03:19,116
MAN: Let down your hair.

17
00:03:22,036 --> 00:03:23,621
You do not pass for a boy.

18
00:03:27,124 --> 00:03:28,584
Let down your hair.

19
00:03:32,087 --> 00:03:33,797
(BREATHES HEAVILY)

20
00:03:40,220 --> 00:03:41,597
I am not afraid.

21
00:03:43,098 --> 00:03:44,975
Travelers spoke in these hills

22
00:03:44,975 --> 00:03:46,477
of the hero Robin Hood.

23
00:03:47,686 --> 00:03:49,104
He protects the meek.

24
00:03:57,029 --> 00:03:58,489
No one protects the meek.

25
00:04:00,783 --> 00:04:02,368
And he were no hero.

26
00:04:02,368 --> 00:04:05,996
He observed thrice a day
and gave silver to the poor.

27
00:04:05,996 --> 00:04:07,873
And he had the love
of Lady Marian.

28
00:04:07,873 --> 00:04:09,249
Is that not a hero?

29
00:04:09,249 --> 00:04:11,919
Lady Marian were taken
from an old shepherd's tale

30
00:04:11,919 --> 00:04:14,672
by a storyteller,
unfortunately, drunker than I.

31
00:04:15,756 --> 00:04:18,759
GIRL: She were the great love
of Robin Hood.

32
00:04:19,468 --> 00:04:20,511
ROBIN: Mmm.

33
00:04:22,221 --> 00:04:24,765
There is no great love
for one such as him.

34
00:04:28,310 --> 00:04:29,728
I have known
those who met this man,

35
00:04:29,728 --> 00:04:31,480
would you like
to know a secret?

36
00:04:32,773 --> 00:04:33,857
I would.

37
00:04:35,192 --> 00:04:37,236
He never prayed once
in his life.

38
00:04:38,946 --> 00:04:41,615
These tales
spread across the land

39
00:04:41,615 --> 00:04:43,534
are lies upon lies.

40
00:04:45,077 --> 00:04:47,538
He were a murderous brigand,

41
00:04:47,538 --> 00:04:49,748
who perhaps cut the throat
of a sheriff

42
00:04:49,748 --> 00:04:51,834
in a long line of others.

43
00:04:51,834 --> 00:04:54,003
People saw a meaning
where there was none.

44
00:04:55,504 --> 00:04:57,297
He was not a hero.

45
00:04:57,297 --> 00:04:59,550
He robbed and killed
for the joy of it,

46
00:04:59,550 --> 00:05:00,718
nothing more.

47
00:05:06,473 --> 00:05:07,599
Those you met...

48
00:05:09,685 --> 00:05:11,103
did they say he felt remorse

49
00:05:11,103 --> 00:05:12,271
for his crimes?

50
00:05:16,442 --> 00:05:18,360
I suspect he felt tired.

51
00:05:21,697 --> 00:05:25,284
I would say if you met
this villain Robin Hood,

52
00:05:25,284 --> 00:05:26,410
you should make haste away

53
00:05:26,410 --> 00:05:28,662
and keep whatever life
you have left here.

54
00:05:31,498 --> 00:05:33,625
-(BREATHING HEAVILY)
-(CLOTHES FLUTTER)

55
00:05:36,253 --> 00:05:38,338
(FOOTSTEPS RECEDE)

56
00:05:39,631 --> 00:05:42,009
(MELLOW MUSIC PLAYING)

57
00:05:58,358 --> 00:06:01,862
(PRAYING INDISTINCTLY)
...pardon of our sins.

58
00:06:03,739 --> 00:06:05,491
Through Jesus Christ our Lord.

59
00:06:07,117 --> 00:06:09,495
Amen. (BREATHING HEAVILY)

60
00:06:12,414 --> 00:06:14,500
(EERIE MUSIC PLAYING)

61
00:06:33,727 --> 00:06:35,771
(BREATHING HEAVILY)

62
00:07:03,632 --> 00:07:05,050
-(KNIFE SLASHING)
-(GIRL SCREAMING)

63
00:07:07,094 --> 00:07:09,138
(GIRL GURGLES)

64
00:07:12,266 --> 00:07:13,600
(GIRL CONTINUES GURGLING)

65
00:07:15,352 --> 00:07:16,979
Who was your kin?

66
00:07:19,398 --> 00:07:22,025
William Wainwright...
(INHALES SHARPLY)

67
00:07:22,025 --> 00:07:23,944
...of Pickering.
(INHALES SHARPLY)

68
00:07:24,528 --> 00:07:25,821
Pickering?

69
00:07:27,865 --> 00:07:29,158
-Father?
-(GIRL WHEEZING)

70
00:07:29,158 --> 00:07:30,659
(GURGLING)

71
00:07:30,659 --> 00:07:31,827
ROBIN: Grandfather?

72
00:07:37,291 --> 00:07:39,668
You should have bathed,

73
00:07:39,668 --> 00:07:41,420
or waited for the wind
to change.

74
00:07:44,173 --> 00:07:46,175
Or kept your weight
on your back foot.

75
00:07:48,552 --> 00:07:50,053
-That's all.
-(GAGGING)

76
00:07:50,596 --> 00:07:51,722
You did well.

77
00:07:55,767 --> 00:07:57,853
(SOMBER MUSIC PLAYING)

78
00:07:58,604 --> 00:07:59,855
(FLESH SQUELCHES)

79
00:08:01,023 --> 00:08:02,441
(EXHALES SOFTLY)

80
00:08:07,654 --> 00:08:10,407
(WIND BLOWING)

81
00:08:10,407 --> 00:08:12,576
(SOMBER MUSIC
CONTINUES PLAYING)

82
00:08:17,497 --> 00:08:19,583
(BREATHING HEAVILY)

83
00:08:33,305 --> 00:08:35,432
(SOMBER MUSIC
CONTINUES PLAYING)

84
00:09:11,677 --> 00:09:12,803
(MUSIC FADES)

85
00:09:12,803 --> 00:09:14,263
WOMAN: They sing lies.

86
00:09:15,597 --> 00:09:17,432
These foolish common folk.

87
00:09:19,017 --> 00:09:21,645
Lies of his heroism
and goodness.

88
00:09:23,814 --> 00:09:25,357
But blood debts are owed.

89
00:09:27,025 --> 00:09:29,653
And generations
of his victims hunt him.

90
00:09:31,738 --> 00:09:32,864
For in truth...

91
00:09:34,533 --> 00:09:37,286
none were more wicked
and wanton

92
00:09:37,286 --> 00:09:39,830
than the murderous bandit
Robin Hood...

93
00:09:41,707 --> 00:09:43,375
and his Little John.

94
00:09:47,713 --> 00:09:48,880
ROBIN: Speak to Thomas.

95
00:09:50,549 --> 00:09:53,051
He were hanged in the South.

96
00:09:53,051 --> 00:09:54,094
ROBIN: Will?

97
00:09:55,679 --> 00:09:57,055
They gutted him.

98
00:09:58,807 --> 00:10:00,475
I have no one else to ask.

99
00:10:02,769 --> 00:10:03,895
What happened?

100
00:10:04,730 --> 00:10:07,858
(SIGHS) My name is Edward.

101
00:10:08,817 --> 00:10:10,193
ROBIN: Who was Edward?

102
00:10:11,778 --> 00:10:15,073
I met him on the road,
ten summers ago.

103
00:10:15,073 --> 00:10:17,659
His brother died.
And he were going to grow

104
00:10:17,659 --> 00:10:19,619
rye and peas
on his brother's land.

105
00:10:21,788 --> 00:10:23,582
No one knew Edward's face.

106
00:10:25,167 --> 00:10:26,376
So I killed him.

107
00:10:27,669 --> 00:10:28,879
Now I am Edward.

108
00:10:30,380 --> 00:10:31,923
And Margaret's a good wife.

109
00:10:32,924 --> 00:10:34,092
It's a good farm.

110
00:10:35,177 --> 00:10:36,386
What happened?

111
00:10:37,804 --> 00:10:39,639
A family on the land
found me out.

112
00:10:40,474 --> 00:10:43,852
Took my farm.
Took my Margaret.

113
00:10:43,852 --> 00:10:45,270
Tried to take my life.

114
00:10:48,774 --> 00:10:50,817
I cannot take back alone
what is mine.

115
00:10:50,817 --> 00:10:52,069
It's not yours.

116
00:10:54,237 --> 00:10:55,447
EDWARD: It's my family.

117
00:11:00,660 --> 00:11:01,953
I'm tired, John.

118
00:11:03,622 --> 00:11:04,915
Edward.

119
00:11:07,376 --> 00:11:08,668
I'm tired, Edward.

120
00:11:12,964 --> 00:11:16,676
(SIGHS) It would be
a mighty battle.

121
00:11:17,344 --> 00:11:18,720
One for the stories.

122
00:11:25,060 --> 00:11:26,561
They are strong?

123
00:11:26,561 --> 00:11:27,562
Aye.

124
00:11:28,647 --> 00:11:30,857
They come from an old family.

125
00:11:30,857 --> 00:11:32,317
They say from Viking blood.

126
00:11:34,194 --> 00:11:36,446
There's a story
that once their elderfather

127
00:11:36,446 --> 00:11:37,531
fought a bear.

128
00:11:39,366 --> 00:11:41,743
It would be
a mighty battle, Robin.

129
00:11:44,871 --> 00:11:45,997
How many are there?

130
00:11:47,416 --> 00:11:49,292
EDWARD: Four? Five?

131
00:11:49,960 --> 00:11:51,545
Together we can best them.

132
00:11:55,048 --> 00:11:56,341
We'll likely die.

133
00:12:02,889 --> 00:12:08,562
TRAVELER:
♪ As I was walking all alone ♪

134
00:12:08,562 --> 00:12:13,733
♪ I heard two crows
a-making a moan ♪

135
00:12:13,733 --> 00:12:19,698
♪ One said to the other,
did say-o ♪

136
00:12:19,698 --> 00:12:24,035
♪ "Where shall we go
and dine today-o? ♪

137
00:12:24,035 --> 00:12:29,791
♪ "Where shall we go
and dine today?" ♪

138
00:12:29,791 --> 00:12:34,254
♪ In behind
that old turf wall ♪

139
00:12:34,254 --> 00:12:39,509
♪ I sense there lies
a new slain knight ♪

140
00:12:39,509 --> 00:12:45,140
♪ And nobody knows
that he lies there-o ♪

141
00:12:45,140 --> 00:12:49,769
♪ But his hawk, and his hound
and his lady fair-o ♪

142
00:12:49,769 --> 00:12:53,982
♪ His hawk, and his hound
and his lady fair ♪

143
00:13:01,698 --> 00:13:07,287
♪ Many a one for him
shall moan ♪

144
00:13:07,287 --> 00:13:12,501
♪ None shall know
where he has gone ♪

145
00:13:12,501 --> 00:13:19,216
♪ O'er his white bones,
when they are bare-o ♪

146
00:13:19,216 --> 00:13:24,304
♪ Wind shall blow
for evermore-o ♪

147
00:13:24,304 --> 00:13:29,142
♪ Wind shall blow
for evermore ♪

148
00:13:31,061 --> 00:13:32,062
Robin!

149
00:13:33,980 --> 00:13:35,106
Robin!

150
00:13:36,775 --> 00:13:39,069
Bread? (LAUGHS)

151
00:13:40,987 --> 00:13:42,364
This is a good adventure.

152
00:13:44,366 --> 00:13:45,492
A good adventure.

153
00:13:48,954 --> 00:13:51,998
You remember
when we met the potter,

154
00:13:51,998 --> 00:13:55,168
did we leave him naked
or did we kill him?

155
00:13:55,168 --> 00:13:56,962
Oh, I tried to tell the story

156
00:13:56,962 --> 00:13:58,922
and someone said
we left him naked.

157
00:13:59,548 --> 00:14:00,840
But I couldn't remember.

158
00:14:01,800 --> 00:14:03,802
You always told
it well, Robin.

159
00:14:05,804 --> 00:14:07,389
Why would we leave him naked?

160
00:14:08,974 --> 00:14:10,100
I don't know.

161
00:14:12,102 --> 00:14:13,436
It were a jest.

162
00:14:14,771 --> 00:14:15,981
We never met a potter.

163
00:14:16,940 --> 00:14:18,900
What do you mean
we never met him?

164
00:14:18,900 --> 00:14:20,151
ROBIN: I mean,
that never happened.

165
00:14:20,151 --> 00:14:22,237
It's just a story
you heard somewhere.

166
00:14:23,655 --> 00:14:24,739
(SCOFFS)

167
00:14:24,739 --> 00:14:26,157
ROBIN: But if we did meet
a potter,

168
00:14:27,325 --> 00:14:28,702
we killed him.

169
00:14:28,702 --> 00:14:30,745
I'd remember
if it were a story.

170
00:14:30,745 --> 00:14:32,581
(GRUNTS) Here!

171
00:14:33,206 --> 00:14:34,541
Tell me about your wife.

172
00:14:37,419 --> 00:14:38,545
Margaret.

173
00:14:40,589 --> 00:14:42,507
She's a good woman.

174
00:14:42,507 --> 00:14:46,261
She's a very good woman.
She's pious.

175
00:14:46,261 --> 00:14:48,179
She teaches me prayerfulness.

176
00:14:48,179 --> 00:14:49,347
Paint a portrait.

177
00:14:51,308 --> 00:14:52,475
Paint a portrait?

178
00:14:53,935 --> 00:14:56,563
(CHUCKLES) Paint a portrait?

179
00:15:01,026 --> 00:15:05,614
She has hair that's red.

180
00:15:06,323 --> 00:15:07,824
ROBIN: Red like?

181
00:15:07,824 --> 00:15:10,076
Red like fresh blood.

182
00:15:10,076 --> 00:15:12,245
ROBIN: No, not blood.
No, no, no.

183
00:15:12,245 --> 00:15:14,956
Something that's red
and that's good, like her.

184
00:15:16,207 --> 00:15:17,834
Red like... I don't know.

185
00:15:21,838 --> 00:15:23,340
Like the setting sun.

186
00:15:23,340 --> 00:15:26,926
Aye, like
the setting summer sun.

187
00:15:28,511 --> 00:15:30,972
Margaret's a good wife.

188
00:15:30,972 --> 00:15:33,933
With hair that's red
like the setting summer sun.

189
00:15:35,935 --> 00:15:37,103
She cares for me.

190
00:15:38,647 --> 00:15:39,814
And I care for her.

191
00:15:42,609 --> 00:15:44,194
(GRUNTS SOFTLY)

192
00:15:44,861 --> 00:15:45,904
ROBIN: Hmm.

193
00:15:48,365 --> 00:15:52,118
EDWARD: Robin, there are open
lands past the mountains.

194
00:15:54,454 --> 00:15:56,039
Many places to begin again.

195
00:16:01,169 --> 00:16:03,129
No, I do not aim
to begin again.

196
00:16:08,635 --> 00:16:10,261
I aim for a right death.

197
00:16:12,847 --> 00:16:14,974
(DOG BARKING)

198
00:16:14,974 --> 00:16:17,102
(TENSE MUSIC PLAYING)

199
00:16:36,371 --> 00:16:38,957
(WIND BLOWING)

200
00:16:58,226 --> 00:17:00,353
(TENSE MUSIC
CONTINUES PLAYING)

201
00:17:08,903 --> 00:17:10,905
-(MAN YELLS, GROANS)
-(KNIFE SLASHING)

202
00:17:13,700 --> 00:17:15,785
(GAGGING AND GASPING)

203
00:17:34,387 --> 00:17:36,514
(TENSE MUSIC
CONTINUES PLAYING)

204
00:17:51,821 --> 00:17:53,072
-(EDWARD GRUNTS)
-MARGARET: Edward!

205
00:17:53,072 --> 00:17:54,240
HENDRIE'S FATHER:
Go, Hendrie!

206
00:17:54,240 --> 00:17:55,950
MARGARET: Edward!

207
00:17:55,950 --> 00:17:58,244
Go, Hendrie, run! Now!

208
00:17:58,787 --> 00:18:00,580
-Edward!
-Back!

209
00:18:00,580 --> 00:18:02,582
-(HENDRIE PANTING)
-Hendrie, get the others!

210
00:18:03,374 --> 00:18:04,542
MARGARET: Edward!

211
00:18:05,627 --> 00:18:08,087
(PANTING) Edward, my love...

212
00:18:09,672 --> 00:18:11,466
Watch over Little Margaret.

213
00:18:11,466 --> 00:18:12,967
-(MAN YELLS)
-(MARGARET SCREAMS)

214
00:18:12,967 --> 00:18:15,053
(SCREAMING)

215
00:18:16,429 --> 00:18:18,348
(EDWARD AND MAN GRUNTING)

216
00:18:23,520 --> 00:18:25,605
(DRAMATIC MUSIC PLAYING)

217
00:18:26,856 --> 00:18:28,316
(PANTING)

218
00:18:29,901 --> 00:18:31,194
(CRYING)

219
00:18:34,447 --> 00:18:35,490
(GRUNTS)

220
00:18:49,212 --> 00:18:51,256
(MUFFLED POUNDING)

221
00:18:55,510 --> 00:18:56,886
(EDWARD GRUNTING)

222
00:19:02,183 --> 00:19:04,644
(YELLING MANIACALLY)

223
00:19:05,645 --> 00:19:07,272
(BREATHING RAGGEDLY)

224
00:19:11,651 --> 00:19:13,194
(BREATHING HEAVILY)

225
00:19:21,035 --> 00:19:23,121
(EDWARD CRYING SOFTLY)

226
00:19:33,298 --> 00:19:34,841
(SNIFFLES AND GURGLES)

227
00:19:38,094 --> 00:19:39,929
(SOBBING)

228
00:19:48,271 --> 00:19:50,690
(EDWARD SCREAMS)

229
00:19:53,192 --> 00:19:55,278
(CONTINUES SOBBING)

230
00:19:56,112 --> 00:19:57,614
(LITTLE MARGARET WHIMPERS)

231
00:20:06,205 --> 00:20:09,459
Little one, don't worry.

232
00:20:09,459 --> 00:20:13,087
Don't worry.
Come here, come here.

233
00:20:13,087 --> 00:20:15,798
You're safe.
You're safe, daughter.

234
00:20:19,802 --> 00:20:21,971
Let us pray. Let us pray.

235
00:20:24,265 --> 00:20:27,310
O Lord, our God,

236
00:20:27,310 --> 00:20:31,105
grant us grace to desire Thee
with our whole heart.

237
00:20:31,731 --> 00:20:33,733
Come on now, pray.

238
00:20:33,733 --> 00:20:36,903
BOTH: And in finding Thee,
we may love Thee.

239
00:20:36,903 --> 00:20:39,906
And in loving Thee,
we may hate those sins

240
00:20:39,906 --> 00:20:42,533
from which Thou hast
redeemed us.

241
00:20:42,533 --> 00:20:45,536
For the sake of Jesus Christ.
Amen.

242
00:20:47,622 --> 00:20:50,667
Yes. Yes, good.

243
00:20:50,667 --> 00:20:53,461
Good. (SOBS, GRUNTS)

244
00:21:05,181 --> 00:21:06,349
(SHEEP BLEATING)

245
00:21:55,106 --> 00:21:56,190
(BREATHES HEAVILY)

246
00:21:59,569 --> 00:22:00,737
HENDRIE: (WHISPERING)
I'm sorry.

247
00:22:02,739 --> 00:22:03,865
I'm sorry.

248
00:22:06,868 --> 00:22:07,994
I'm sorry.

249
00:22:09,579 --> 00:22:11,748
It were that when I woke.

250
00:22:14,083 --> 00:22:15,752
It were that when I woke.

251
00:22:16,961 --> 00:22:18,713
SHEPHERD: Cousin.

252
00:22:18,713 --> 00:22:20,548
It were that when I woke.

253
00:22:22,133 --> 00:22:23,426
I'm sorry.

254
00:22:24,886 --> 00:22:30,141
TRAVELER: ♪ The wind
between the north and east ♪

255
00:22:30,141 --> 00:22:33,519
♪ Blowed every chill
and cold ♪

256
00:22:35,396 --> 00:22:40,151
♪ Or coldly blowed to me,
at least ♪

257
00:22:40,151 --> 00:22:43,362
♪ My clothes were thin
and old ♪

258
00:22:44,489 --> 00:22:49,160
♪ The grass all dropping wet
with dew ♪

259
00:22:49,160 --> 00:22:51,996
♪ Low bent their tiny spears ♪

260
00:22:53,372 --> 00:22:57,460
♪ The lowly daisy bended too ♪

261
00:22:58,336 --> 00:23:03,216
♪ More lowly with my tears ♪

262
00:23:05,426 --> 00:23:10,431
♪ Oh, luckless youth
to sorrow born ♪

263
00:23:11,182 --> 00:23:13,935
♪ Shun'd son of poverty ♪

264
00:23:15,520 --> 00:23:20,191
♪ The world made gamely
sport and scorn ♪

265
00:23:20,942 --> 00:23:23,236
♪ And grinning infamy ♪

266
00:23:24,695 --> 00:23:28,825
♪ Unequalled though
my sorrow seems ♪

267
00:23:28,825 --> 00:23:32,328
♪ But great indeed they are ♪

268
00:23:32,328 --> 00:23:36,165
♪ Oh hear my sorrows
for my stream ♪

269
00:23:37,333 --> 00:23:41,504
♪ You'll find an equal there ♪

270
00:23:43,131 --> 00:23:46,551
♪ The land and Eddings
are no more ♪

271
00:23:47,802 --> 00:23:50,638
♪ The pastures too are gone ♪

272
00:23:52,473 --> 00:23:57,311
♪ The greens, the meadows
and the moors ♪

273
00:23:57,311 --> 00:24:00,148
♪ Are all cut up and gone ♪

274
00:24:00,148 --> 00:24:02,400
ELDERFATHER: We cry
to Thee, O Lord.

275
00:24:04,068 --> 00:24:06,445
Do Thou have mercy upon us?

276
00:24:07,905 --> 00:24:09,824
FAMILY:
And grant forgiveness.

277
00:24:09,824 --> 00:24:14,036
ELDERFATHER: O King of heaven,
and everlasting Lord,

278
00:24:14,036 --> 00:24:18,040
receive our prayers
which we pour forth.

279
00:24:19,417 --> 00:24:21,377
FAMILY:
And grant forgiveness.

280
00:24:21,377 --> 00:24:26,048
ELDERFATHER: Visit the sick,
bring forth the captive,

281
00:24:26,048 --> 00:24:29,468
help the widow and the orphan.

282
00:24:30,970 --> 00:24:33,556
FAMILY:
And grant forgiveness.

283
00:24:33,556 --> 00:24:38,519
ELDERFATHER: We have sinned
and departed from Thee.

284
00:24:38,519 --> 00:24:43,816
Wilt Thou, the Redeemer
of all, save us?

285
00:24:44,942 --> 00:24:46,986
FAMILY:
And grant forgiveness.

286
00:24:46,986 --> 00:24:49,864
Have mercy on the penitent

287
00:24:49,864 --> 00:24:53,993
and wash away
the stains of sin.

288
00:24:55,661 --> 00:24:57,705
FAMILY:
And grant forgiveness.

289
00:24:59,498 --> 00:25:01,209
ALL: Amen.

290
00:25:06,547 --> 00:25:08,633
(EERIE MUSIC PLAYING)

291
00:25:08,633 --> 00:25:10,718
(MUFFLED FIRE CRACKLING)

292
00:25:16,265 --> 00:25:17,975
(DISTANT THUD)

293
00:25:25,358 --> 00:25:27,443
(DISTANT MAN SHOUTS)

294
00:25:28,986 --> 00:25:30,321
(GRUNTS SOFTLY)

295
00:25:33,616 --> 00:25:35,660
(FLAMES WHOOSHING)

296
00:25:46,754 --> 00:25:48,172
MAN 1: Kill him!

297
00:25:48,839 --> 00:25:50,633
(MAN 2 YELLS)

298
00:25:55,304 --> 00:25:56,555
MAN: Godwyn!

299
00:25:58,641 --> 00:26:00,726
-(GRUNTING)
-(ROBIN YELLS)

300
00:26:02,728 --> 00:26:05,231
(ROBIN COUGHING)

301
00:26:05,940 --> 00:26:06,941
(SCREAMS)

302
00:26:09,485 --> 00:26:11,112
(SCREAMING)

303
00:26:13,155 --> 00:26:14,407
(PANTING)

304
00:26:15,616 --> 00:26:18,077
(SCREAMS)

305
00:26:18,077 --> 00:26:20,413
(ROBIN GRUNTS, COUGHS)

306
00:26:21,372 --> 00:26:24,000
-(ROBIN GRUNTS)
-(MAN SCREAMING)

307
00:26:25,918 --> 00:26:27,712
(MAN WHIMPERING LOUDLY)

308
00:26:32,717 --> 00:26:34,051
MAN: No!

309
00:26:34,051 --> 00:26:35,344
(MAN SCREAMS)

310
00:26:36,637 --> 00:26:38,556
(GROANS)

311
00:26:39,932 --> 00:26:41,434
(ROBIN COUGHS)

312
00:26:42,935 --> 00:26:44,395
(MAN GRUNTING)

313
00:26:45,104 --> 00:26:46,522
(BOTH YELLING)

314
00:26:48,232 --> 00:26:49,775
(MAN SCREAMING)

315
00:26:56,365 --> 00:26:57,992
(ROBIN PANTING)

316
00:26:57,992 --> 00:27:00,036
(MAN GROANING)

317
00:27:05,458 --> 00:27:07,460
-(CONTINUES PANTING)
-(MAN GRUNTING IN PANIC)

318
00:27:14,342 --> 00:27:15,801
(BOTH SCREAM)

319
00:27:19,430 --> 00:27:21,349
(ROBIN GASPS, GROANS)

320
00:27:22,892 --> 00:27:24,060
(ROBIN GRUNTS)

321
00:27:25,603 --> 00:27:28,022
(MAN SCREAMING)

322
00:27:30,524 --> 00:27:32,026
(YELLS)

323
00:27:33,235 --> 00:27:35,321
(MAN WHEEZING LOUDLY)

324
00:27:52,797 --> 00:27:54,090
(ROBIN COUGHS)

325
00:27:57,093 --> 00:27:59,178
(MAN GRUNTING WEAKLY)

326
00:28:01,055 --> 00:28:03,682
No. Please. Please.

327
00:28:03,682 --> 00:28:06,769
(CRYING) Please, please!
(SCREAMING)

328
00:28:06,769 --> 00:28:09,688
-(ROBIN YELLS, GRUNTS)
-(FLESH SQUELCHES)

329
00:28:10,231 --> 00:28:11,565
(ROBIN COUGHING)

330
00:28:18,697 --> 00:28:20,699
-(EDWARD SCREAMS)
-(POUNDING ON DOOR)

331
00:28:27,498 --> 00:28:28,541
(EDWARD SCREAMS)

332
00:28:30,584 --> 00:28:33,504
(EDWARD AND
LITTLE MARGARET COUGHING)

333
00:28:36,882 --> 00:28:39,927
-EDWARD: Margaret? Margaret!
-ROBIN: No, no.

334
00:28:39,927 --> 00:28:41,887
No! (BREATHING HEAVILY)

335
00:28:41,887 --> 00:28:43,139
(LITTLE MARGARET COUGHS)

336
00:28:44,140 --> 00:28:46,434
ROBIN: John! Don't!

337
00:28:51,689 --> 00:28:53,983
ELDERFATHER: Little John!

338
00:29:01,073 --> 00:29:05,536
Twenty winters ago,
you murdered my cousin,

339
00:29:05,536 --> 00:29:07,538
Walter of Hathersage.

340
00:29:08,914 --> 00:29:11,834
You have murdered my sons

341
00:29:11,834 --> 00:29:13,419
and my grandson.

342
00:29:14,462 --> 00:29:15,671
As wergild...

343
00:29:16,255 --> 00:29:19,216
I accept your life...

344
00:29:19,216 --> 00:29:22,219
and the life of your daughter.

345
00:29:22,219 --> 00:29:23,471
(ROBIN GRUNTS ANGRILY)

346
00:29:27,183 --> 00:29:29,477
(BREATHING HEAVILY)

347
00:29:30,603 --> 00:29:31,687
(GRUNTS ANGRILY)

348
00:29:36,275 --> 00:29:37,318
(ROBIN GRUNTING)

349
00:29:38,611 --> 00:29:39,653
(ROBIN GROANS)

350
00:30:00,674 --> 00:30:01,759
(MACE SWOOSHING)

351
00:30:03,802 --> 00:30:05,346
(BOTH GRUNTING)

352
00:30:07,014 --> 00:30:08,057
(ROBIN GROANS)

353
00:30:11,393 --> 00:30:12,686
(ROBIN GRUNTING)

354
00:30:16,190 --> 00:30:17,441
(ELDERFATHER GRUNTS)

355
00:30:20,528 --> 00:30:22,571
-(ROBIN GRUNTING)
-(ELDERFATHER GROANING)

356
00:30:24,031 --> 00:30:25,074
(ROBIN GROANS)

357
00:30:30,746 --> 00:30:31,789
(ROBIN GRUNTS)

358
00:30:35,376 --> 00:30:36,502
-(HELMET CLANGS)
-(ROBIN GRUNTS)

359
00:30:37,878 --> 00:30:39,129
(GROANS)

360
00:30:40,756 --> 00:30:43,801
-(ELDERFATHER YELLS)
-(ROBIN SCREAMING)

361
00:30:47,179 --> 00:30:49,056
(GASPING)

362
00:31:00,234 --> 00:31:02,570
-(ELDERFATHER GRUNTS)
-(SCREAMS)

363
00:31:06,615 --> 00:31:09,368
(ROBIN GROANS,
GURGLES AND COUGHS)

364
00:31:13,539 --> 00:31:14,540
(GAGS)

365
00:31:22,214 --> 00:31:24,258
(VOICES WHISPERING
INDISTINCTLY)

366
00:31:29,888 --> 00:31:31,473
MAN: (WHISPERING)
...heinous crimes...

367
00:31:33,642 --> 00:31:37,104
...slayer of man,
woman and child...

368
00:31:39,732 --> 00:31:40,774
(BLOW LANDS)

369
00:31:44,612 --> 00:31:46,155
No...

370
00:31:51,744 --> 00:31:53,537
No...

371
00:31:53,537 --> 00:31:57,750
(ECHOING) No! No!
No! No! No!

372
00:31:57,750 --> 00:31:59,668
(YELLING) No!

373
00:32:00,628 --> 00:32:02,379
No!

374
00:32:04,632 --> 00:32:06,925
(CART RATTLING)

375
00:32:09,428 --> 00:32:11,555
(ROBIN BREATHING WEAKLY)

376
00:32:37,998 --> 00:32:40,084
(WIND BLOWING)

377
00:32:50,719 --> 00:32:52,721
(KNIFE SIZZLING)

378
00:32:55,891 --> 00:32:57,393
(FLESH SIZZLING)

379
00:32:57,393 --> 00:32:58,894
(ROBIN SCREAMING)

380
00:33:07,611 --> 00:33:08,654
(ROBIN GROANS)

381
00:33:08,654 --> 00:33:10,698
(INDISTINCT VOICES
CONTINUE WHISPERING)

382
00:33:12,825 --> 00:33:13,992
EDWARD: Robin?

383
00:33:14,993 --> 00:33:18,372
I've heard stories
of a Priory hidden away.

384
00:33:19,998 --> 00:33:21,458
There's a Prioress there

385
00:33:21,458 --> 00:33:23,502
who's older
than anyone remembers...

386
00:33:24,920 --> 00:33:26,463
she has healing magic.

387
00:33:27,965 --> 00:33:29,675
I'll take you to her.

388
00:33:29,675 --> 00:33:31,552
She'll get you well again.

389
00:33:32,636 --> 00:33:34,596
I've heard incredible stories.

390
00:33:36,348 --> 00:33:38,434
(MELLOW MUSIC PLAYING)

391
00:33:46,942 --> 00:33:48,986
(WAVES SPLASHING)

392
00:34:19,558 --> 00:34:21,643
(WIND BLOWING)

393
00:34:29,818 --> 00:34:31,236
(BANGING)

394
00:34:31,987 --> 00:34:34,072
(BREATHING SHALLOW)

395
00:34:35,574 --> 00:34:38,368
Robin, we must leave you.

396
00:34:39,870 --> 00:34:41,121
They'll be hunting us.

397
00:34:42,873 --> 00:34:44,958
(INDISTINCT WHISPERING)

398
00:34:47,085 --> 00:34:49,213
(CONTINUES
BREATHING SHALLOW)

399
00:34:54,885 --> 00:34:56,345
(SILENCE)

400
00:35:18,116 --> 00:35:19,618
WOMAN: (ECHOING)
Look at the snow...

401
00:35:22,663 --> 00:35:24,373
it's strange
for it to fall this late.

402
00:35:28,669 --> 00:35:30,337
But it won't stay
on the ground...

403
00:35:33,549 --> 00:35:35,259
and the long days
will soon some.

404
00:35:41,515 --> 00:35:44,017
(GASPS, GROANS)

405
00:35:46,311 --> 00:35:48,230
(SEAGULLS CALLING)

406
00:35:51,358 --> 00:35:53,360
(EXHALES)

407
00:35:55,320 --> 00:35:56,613
(GRUNTS)

408
00:36:00,033 --> 00:36:02,119
(FOOTSTEPS APPROACHING)

409
00:36:14,590 --> 00:36:15,924
(DOOR CLOSES)

410
00:36:28,228 --> 00:36:29,646
WOMAN:
A man brought you here.

411
00:36:31,148 --> 00:36:32,649
He sealed
your wounds with fire

412
00:36:32,649 --> 00:36:34,693
-and he did not do a poor job.
-(GROANS SOFTLY)

413
00:36:36,278 --> 00:36:37,905
You have been here six days.

414
00:36:39,406 --> 00:36:41,158
You have wounds
in your abdomen.

415
00:36:42,117 --> 00:36:44,453
Ribs and a leg badly broken.

416
00:36:46,705 --> 00:36:48,582
Do you understand my words?

417
00:36:52,794 --> 00:36:54,880
Where is this?

418
00:36:54,880 --> 00:36:57,007
WOMAN: You're at
the Priory of St. Clement.

419
00:36:57,007 --> 00:36:58,425
We're a place for you to heal.

420
00:36:59,509 --> 00:37:01,136
Wha... What town?

421
00:37:02,054 --> 00:37:03,305
There is no town.

422
00:37:05,098 --> 00:37:06,934
Do you know the story of Saul?

423
00:37:09,061 --> 00:37:10,312
What?

424
00:37:10,312 --> 00:37:13,482
It goes, there once
was a man, Saul...

425
00:37:13,482 --> 00:37:16,860
a breather of menaces
against disciples of the Lord.

426
00:37:17,778 --> 00:37:19,529
He came
to the Prince of Priests

427
00:37:19,529 --> 00:37:21,907
and asked of him
letters into Damascus,

428
00:37:21,907 --> 00:37:23,408
to the synagogues.

429
00:37:23,408 --> 00:37:24,743
That if he came across

430
00:37:24,743 --> 00:37:26,745
any man or woman of this life,
he should lead them bound

431
00:37:26,745 --> 00:37:28,080
-and...
-(ROBIN GRUNTS)

432
00:37:29,456 --> 00:37:31,333
That is all we need to do.

433
00:37:32,584 --> 00:37:34,169
-Just a few drops.
-(BLOOD DRIPPING)

434
00:37:36,755 --> 00:37:38,340
It was not too painful?

435
00:37:40,592 --> 00:37:41,718
It were not.

436
00:37:46,264 --> 00:37:47,474
I'm Sister Brigid.

437
00:37:48,892 --> 00:37:50,268
What's your name?

438
00:37:52,312 --> 00:37:53,355
Randolph.

439
00:37:57,818 --> 00:38:00,320
Well, I owe you
an apology, Randolph.

440
00:38:01,238 --> 00:38:02,698
What apology?

441
00:38:04,741 --> 00:38:06,576
You begged for me
to let you die.

442
00:38:08,704 --> 00:38:09,913
And I did not.

443
00:38:12,290 --> 00:38:13,417
You said it was right.

444
00:38:13,417 --> 00:38:14,543
I were with fever.

445
00:38:16,712 --> 00:38:18,338
Well, your fever has passed.

446
00:38:20,007 --> 00:38:21,174
Rest now, Randolph.

447
00:38:44,740 --> 00:38:47,826
MAN: Your body
is covered in scars.

448
00:38:48,535 --> 00:38:50,620
(ROBIN GASPING SOFTLY)

449
00:39:04,926 --> 00:39:07,304
Do you mean anyone
at this Priory harm?

450
00:39:11,308 --> 00:39:12,392
I do not.

451
00:39:22,736 --> 00:39:25,697
THE LEPER: Will any harm
follow you here?

452
00:39:27,699 --> 00:39:28,825
It will not.

453
00:39:38,085 --> 00:39:40,962
THE LEPER: (SOFTLY)
You will do them no harm.

454
00:39:55,060 --> 00:39:56,103
(DOOR CLOSES)

455
00:40:00,107 --> 00:40:01,566
(BIRDS CHIRPING)

456
00:40:13,203 --> 00:40:15,163
SISTER BRIGID:
Now back to the bed.

457
00:40:15,163 --> 00:40:17,833
(SLOW, LIMPING FOOTSTEPS)

458
00:40:22,754 --> 00:40:24,881
(ROBIN GRUNTING)

459
00:40:27,634 --> 00:40:29,302
(BREATHING HEAVILY)

460
00:40:30,387 --> 00:40:31,763
Now back to the door.

461
00:40:37,435 --> 00:40:39,479
(ROBIN GRUNTING)

462
00:41:00,000 --> 00:41:01,168
SISTER BRIGID:
Now open the door.

463
00:41:01,168 --> 00:41:02,711
(ROBIN BREATHING HEAVILY)

464
00:41:09,259 --> 00:41:11,344
(INTRIGUING MUSIC PLAYING)

465
00:41:20,478 --> 00:41:22,022
Go as far as you'd like.

466
00:41:25,483 --> 00:41:27,611
(CONTINUES BREATHING HEAVILY)

467
00:41:36,870 --> 00:41:38,997
(BREATHING HEAVILY, GRUNTING)

468
00:42:01,102 --> 00:42:03,021
(BIRD CHIRPING)

469
00:42:04,689 --> 00:42:06,775
(SERENE MUSIC PLAYING)

470
00:42:16,826 --> 00:42:18,912
(DISTANT ANIMAL CALLING)

471
00:42:28,046 --> 00:42:30,090
(BIRDS CHIRPING)

472
00:42:47,941 --> 00:42:50,110
(CHILDREN LAUGHING,
CHATTERING)

473
00:42:54,656 --> 00:42:56,741
(GOATS BLEATING)

474
00:43:03,498 --> 00:43:05,583
(CHATTERING CONTINUES)

475
00:43:10,005 --> 00:43:11,923
WOMAN: William,
careful, please.

476
00:43:11,923 --> 00:43:14,175
(BOY SPEAKING INDISTINCTLY)

477
00:43:16,303 --> 00:43:18,430
(GROANS, BREATHES HEAVILY)

478
00:43:20,098 --> 00:43:21,266
SISTER BRIGID:
Can you row yet?

479
00:43:24,144 --> 00:43:26,187
There is a boat
on the west shore.

480
00:43:26,187 --> 00:43:28,690
(PANTING)

481
00:43:28,690 --> 00:43:29,858
That's all right.

482
00:43:31,318 --> 00:43:33,737
You can stay here
if you help in the orchard.

483
00:43:33,737 --> 00:43:35,613
Oh, I'm... not a farmer.

484
00:43:36,614 --> 00:43:38,241
Then you know not how to hunt.

485
00:43:38,867 --> 00:43:40,327
Aye, I know how to hunt.

486
00:43:41,536 --> 00:43:44,539
There is some game
on this island.

487
00:43:44,539 --> 00:43:48,126
But no, you cannot hunt.
You cannot even draw a bow.

488
00:43:48,126 --> 00:43:50,462
-I can draw my bow.
-It's mostly rabbits.

489
00:43:50,462 --> 00:43:53,089
-Do you know how to trap?
-I know how to trap.

490
00:43:54,758 --> 00:43:56,968
Good. But no more
than six traps

491
00:43:56,968 --> 00:43:58,887
or the population
will not sustain.

492
00:43:59,637 --> 00:44:01,181
Can you manage six traps?

493
00:44:05,602 --> 00:44:07,020
I can manage six traps.

494
00:44:09,314 --> 00:44:10,398
Good.

495
00:44:11,775 --> 00:44:14,611
Then the rest of your time,
you may help in the orchard.

496
00:44:19,074 --> 00:44:21,493
-(WAVES SPLASHING)
-(SEAGULLS CALLING)

497
00:44:31,044 --> 00:44:32,087
(GRUNTS)

498
00:44:33,171 --> 00:44:34,297
(GROANS)

499
00:44:38,343 --> 00:44:39,677
(GRUNTS)

500
00:44:40,678 --> 00:44:42,597
(GROANS)

501
00:44:43,640 --> 00:44:45,392
(BREATHING HEAVILY)

502
00:44:50,939 --> 00:44:52,565
THE LEPER: Are you
trying to flee?

503
00:44:56,653 --> 00:44:57,862
You are quiet.

504
00:44:59,614 --> 00:45:01,783
THE LEPER: I am quiet
and slow.

505
00:45:03,243 --> 00:45:06,830
You are quiet,
but you need to be slow.

506
00:45:06,830 --> 00:45:08,039
You're a cripple.

507
00:45:08,039 --> 00:45:09,457
Aye, you're a leper.

508
00:45:10,667 --> 00:45:14,337
THE LEPER: It is all right
to be slow. We are safe here.

509
00:45:14,337 --> 00:45:16,923
ROBIN: That's quite a thing
to say. You know that?

510
00:45:16,923 --> 00:45:19,134
THE LEPER: I know that
because she has made us safe.

511
00:45:19,134 --> 00:45:20,468
ROBIN: Oh, your Prioress?

512
00:45:22,887 --> 00:45:24,139
THE LEPER: She were like us.

513
00:45:25,181 --> 00:45:29,227
She knew great sorrow.
Great pain.

514
00:45:30,103 --> 00:45:31,229
Widowed young.

515
00:45:33,398 --> 00:45:34,983
But through that sorrow,

516
00:45:34,983 --> 00:45:37,235
she found the strength
to heal us.

517
00:45:37,235 --> 00:45:39,821
Well, you do not look
like you've been healed.

518
00:45:39,821 --> 00:45:41,281
(THE LEPER CHUCKLES)

519
00:45:43,283 --> 00:45:45,160
The Prioress once told me
a story

520
00:45:45,160 --> 00:45:46,995
of a great philosopher...

521
00:45:48,246 --> 00:45:50,748
the greatest philosopher
of his time.

522
00:45:51,791 --> 00:45:55,211
But one day,
his king turned against him.

523
00:45:55,211 --> 00:45:58,298
Threw him in a dungeon
to be tortured and executed.

524
00:45:59,799 --> 00:46:01,843
But in his misery,

525
00:46:01,843 --> 00:46:04,929
when he had nothing,
an angel came

526
00:46:04,929 --> 00:46:07,223
to the philosopher
and she spoke to him.

527
00:46:08,308 --> 00:46:10,310
And from her,

528
00:46:10,310 --> 00:46:12,854
he learned
that it did not matter

529
00:46:12,854 --> 00:46:15,482
if this whole world
were misery...

530
00:46:17,275 --> 00:46:19,194
if all he had were death.

531
00:46:20,987 --> 00:46:22,906
Because he had one thing

532
00:46:22,906 --> 00:46:25,241
that could never be
touched or taken.

533
00:46:27,911 --> 00:46:29,162
He had his mind.

534
00:46:31,122 --> 00:46:32,248
And in his mind...

535
00:46:34,083 --> 00:46:35,293
he had God.

536
00:46:37,962 --> 00:46:41,549
It's never too late
to find peace. (GRUNTS)

537
00:46:42,425 --> 00:46:43,676
What are you doing?

538
00:46:43,676 --> 00:46:46,638
THE LEPER: (GROANS) Ah,
I row across every day.

539
00:46:46,638 --> 00:46:48,556
Your friend was strong enough
to take you,

540
00:46:48,556 --> 00:46:50,433
but some are not,
so I ferry them.

541
00:46:50,433 --> 00:46:53,311
-Will you take me across?
-I will not.

542
00:46:53,311 --> 00:46:56,439
You can row yourself
when you're stronger.

543
00:46:56,439 --> 00:46:59,359
For now, work at your tasks.
Trapping, I hear.

544
00:46:59,359 --> 00:47:01,569
-I will hunt.
-No, you can't hunt.

545
00:47:03,446 --> 00:47:04,989
(THE LEPER GRUNTS)

546
00:47:06,991 --> 00:47:08,701
ROBIN: Leper,

547
00:47:08,701 --> 00:47:10,870
what happened
to that great philosopher?

548
00:47:11,996 --> 00:47:14,874
He were tortured
and executed, of course.

549
00:47:16,459 --> 00:47:17,585
Of course.

550
00:47:22,882 --> 00:47:24,259
(LAUGHTER AND CHATTER)

551
00:47:24,259 --> 00:47:26,844
THE LEPER: ♪ Sumer is icumen
In loude sing cuccu! ♪

552
00:47:26,844 --> 00:47:29,389
♪ Groweth seed
and bloweth meed ♪

553
00:47:29,389 --> 00:47:31,391
♪ And springth the wode now ♪

554
00:47:31,391 --> 00:47:33,643
♪ Sing cuccu ♪

555
00:47:33,643 --> 00:47:36,980
♪ Ewe bleteth after lamb
Loweth after calve cow ♪

556
00:47:36,980 --> 00:47:39,065
(MELLOW MUSIC PLAYING)

557
00:48:16,144 --> 00:48:18,104
(MUSIC CONTINUES)

558
00:48:35,288 --> 00:48:37,373
(BREATHING SHARPLY)

559
00:48:40,960 --> 00:48:43,546
-(GRUNTING)
-(BOWSTRING STRETCHES)

560
00:48:45,590 --> 00:48:47,467
-(BOWSTRING TWANGS)
-(BREATHING HEAVILY)

561
00:48:51,095 --> 00:48:53,056
(GRUNTING)

562
00:48:53,056 --> 00:48:55,058
(BREATHING HEAVILY)

563
00:48:57,727 --> 00:48:58,728
(GROANS)

564
00:48:59,979 --> 00:49:01,606
(GRUNTING)

565
00:49:02,482 --> 00:49:04,150
-(GROANS)
-(ARROW THUDS)

566
00:49:04,150 --> 00:49:05,360
-(YELLS)
-(BOW CLATTERS)

567
00:49:05,360 --> 00:49:07,445
(BREATHING HEAVILY)

568
00:49:09,489 --> 00:49:11,240
(BOY YELLING INDISTINCTLY)

569
00:49:18,289 --> 00:49:20,625
ROBIN: Oi! Oi!

570
00:49:20,625 --> 00:49:22,418
The Leper has brought someone!

571
00:49:22,960 --> 00:49:24,754
From the far shore!

572
00:49:56,828 --> 00:49:58,329
(LITTLE MARGARET
BREATHES HEAVILY)

573
00:50:04,669 --> 00:50:05,962
LITTLE MARGARET: Papa's dead.

574
00:50:08,965 --> 00:50:10,675
(SOMBER MUSIC PLAYING)

575
00:50:30,319 --> 00:50:32,488
(GRASS RUSTLING)

576
00:50:32,488 --> 00:50:34,532
(BIRDS CHIRPING)

577
00:50:36,492 --> 00:50:38,578
(RABBIT'S LEGS THUMPING)

578
00:50:54,761 --> 00:50:56,554
(LEGS THUMPING FRANTICALLY)

579
00:50:59,599 --> 00:51:01,100
-(BONES CRACK)
-(THUMPING STOPS)

580
00:51:03,186 --> 00:51:04,771
(RECEDING FOOTSTEPS)

581
00:51:10,860 --> 00:51:12,028
(CROW CAWS)

582
00:51:14,113 --> 00:51:15,198
(ROBIN GROANS SOFTLY)

583
00:51:21,621 --> 00:51:23,122
How's the girl?

584
00:51:36,511 --> 00:51:38,429
THE LEPER: Let me show you
my trees.

585
00:51:48,648 --> 00:51:51,901
These are the elderberries.

586
00:51:51,901 --> 00:51:54,529
They harvest
late in the summer.

587
00:51:54,529 --> 00:51:56,948
ROBIN: Do they know
what you were before this?

588
00:51:56,948 --> 00:51:59,075
THE LEPER: Oh, you'll have
to favor this side

589
00:51:59,075 --> 00:52:00,117
for my hearing.

590
00:52:02,662 --> 00:52:05,248
Pears, I harvest just after.

591
00:52:08,459 --> 00:52:10,545
And what were I before this?

592
00:52:10,545 --> 00:52:12,171
ROBIN: Oh,
a sellsword at best.

593
00:52:12,171 --> 00:52:14,590
THE LEPER: (CHUCKLING)
Aye, at best, certainly.

594
00:52:15,591 --> 00:52:16,968
We have a way about us!

595
00:52:18,427 --> 00:52:19,929
You remember the trees?

596
00:52:19,929 --> 00:52:22,223
-Pears and elderberries.
-THE LEPER: Wonderful.

597
00:52:25,268 --> 00:52:26,477
Some of them know.

598
00:52:27,812 --> 00:52:31,023
But the Prioress does not care
who we were.

599
00:52:33,109 --> 00:52:35,570
And the apples harvest
in the autumn.

600
00:52:36,946 --> 00:52:40,074
ROBIN: Did she, uh,
speak of what happened to her?

601
00:52:41,617 --> 00:52:43,744
THE LEPER: Well, the Prioress
is tending to the girl.

602
00:52:43,744 --> 00:52:46,581
You should speak with her,
if you are concerned.

603
00:52:50,084 --> 00:52:51,252
What were the trees?

604
00:52:53,129 --> 00:52:55,590
ROBIN: Pears, apples
and elderberries.

605
00:52:57,592 --> 00:52:58,759
THE LEPER: Wonderful.

606
00:52:59,719 --> 00:53:00,761
(DOOR CLOSES)

607
00:53:10,438 --> 00:53:12,189
SISTER BRIGID: This will be
your last letting.

608
00:53:13,733 --> 00:53:15,902
Though your body
still has much healing.

609
00:53:19,655 --> 00:53:20,823
You're tired.

610
00:53:22,033 --> 00:53:23,159
I am.

611
00:53:24,410 --> 00:53:25,912
But I have been tired before.

612
00:53:28,080 --> 00:53:29,415
The child does not sleep.

613
00:53:32,960 --> 00:53:34,295
What happened to her?

614
00:53:36,005 --> 00:53:37,131
Terrible things.

615
00:53:40,259 --> 00:53:43,554
-She told you her story?
-No, she does not speak.

616
00:53:43,554 --> 00:53:45,765
She does not eat
and she does not sleep.

617
00:53:50,102 --> 00:53:52,313
-(SKIN PRICKS)
-(BLOOD DRIPPING)

618
00:53:53,439 --> 00:53:54,690
What is her name?

619
00:54:02,448 --> 00:54:03,574
Margaret.

620
00:54:06,661 --> 00:54:08,079
Who is she?

621
00:54:10,081 --> 00:54:13,084
I met her father. A farmer.

622
00:54:14,210 --> 00:54:16,087
SISTER BRIGID: Well,
he's dead.

623
00:54:16,087 --> 00:54:17,171
It seems.

624
00:54:17,171 --> 00:54:18,673
And the mother?

625
00:54:18,673 --> 00:54:21,509
-Ah, I think dead.
-How?

626
00:54:21,509 --> 00:54:23,511
I don't know. Dead past.

627
00:54:24,679 --> 00:54:25,721
Slain?

628
00:54:26,973 --> 00:54:28,057
Perhaps.

629
00:54:33,521 --> 00:54:34,814
You lie very well.

630
00:54:35,940 --> 00:54:37,608
(BLOOD STOPS DRIPPING)

631
00:54:44,323 --> 00:54:45,491
Did the child see?

632
00:54:48,202 --> 00:54:49,620
She could have.

633
00:54:52,164 --> 00:54:54,000
That is much
for a child to see.

634
00:54:56,919 --> 00:54:58,254
(SIGHS)

635
00:55:04,135 --> 00:55:05,594
I'm sorry for your friend.

636
00:55:10,224 --> 00:55:11,350
Thank you.

637
00:55:12,727 --> 00:55:14,895
Would you like me
to pray for him?

638
00:55:19,108 --> 00:55:20,443
As you wish.

639
00:55:24,030 --> 00:55:25,281
What was his name?

640
00:55:29,827 --> 00:55:30,953
Edward.

641
00:55:33,205 --> 00:55:34,373
SISTER BRIGID: Edward.

642
00:55:37,668 --> 00:55:39,128
Be not your heart afraid,

643
00:55:39,128 --> 00:55:40,463
for ye believe in God

644
00:55:40,463 --> 00:55:41,756
and ye believe in me.

645
00:55:43,632 --> 00:55:46,427
In the house of my father,
there are many rooms.

646
00:55:47,887 --> 00:55:50,973
And when I make ready
for you a place,

647
00:55:50,973 --> 00:55:53,309
I shall come
and take thee to myself.

648
00:55:54,643 --> 00:55:57,521
For where I am,
He may be also.

649
00:56:00,066 --> 00:56:02,276
And whither I go,
He shall know the way.

650
00:56:05,404 --> 00:56:07,114
(INDISTINCT CONVERSATIONS)

651
00:56:08,115 --> 00:56:10,493
(BOY SCREAMING, CRYING LOUDLY)

652
00:56:14,121 --> 00:56:16,290
(BOY CONTINUES SCREAMING
AND CRYING)

653
00:56:23,422 --> 00:56:25,174
-THE LEPER: She has a knife.
-SISTER BRIGID: Margaret!

654
00:56:25,174 --> 00:56:26,675
She stabbed me!

655
00:56:26,675 --> 00:56:28,177
SISTER BRIGID: Margaret!

656
00:56:30,387 --> 00:56:32,473
-(BOY CONTINUES CRYING)
-It's all right.

657
00:56:32,473 --> 00:56:33,682
(LITTLE MARGARET
BREATHING HEAVILY)

658
00:56:33,682 --> 00:56:35,851
Margaret, it's...
it's all right.

659
00:56:35,851 --> 00:56:37,686
-Margaret, it's all right.
-(LITTLE MARGARET CRIES)

660
00:56:40,689 --> 00:56:42,441
THE LEPER: She don't know
this island.

661
00:56:42,441 --> 00:56:44,151
There are boar about.

662
00:56:45,903 --> 00:56:47,238
(SOMBER MUSIC PLAYING)

663
00:56:47,238 --> 00:56:48,697
-THE LEPER: Margaret!
-SISTER BRIGID: Margaret!

664
00:56:48,697 --> 00:56:50,533
-(WIND BLOWING)
-SISTER BRIGID: Margaret!

665
00:56:50,533 --> 00:56:53,536
-WOMAN: Margaret!
-SISTER BRIGID: Margaret!

666
00:56:53,536 --> 00:56:55,037
WOMAN: Margaret!

667
00:56:57,039 --> 00:56:59,083
(WIND WHOOSHING)

668
00:57:01,293 --> 00:57:03,129
(FOREBODING MUSIC PLAYING)

669
00:57:25,609 --> 00:57:27,236
(THUNDER RUMBLING)

670
00:57:52,511 --> 00:57:54,680
(BREATHING HEAVILY)

671
00:58:25,711 --> 00:58:26,754
(GRUNTS SOFTLY)

672
00:58:40,768 --> 00:58:41,810
Oi.

673
00:58:44,480 --> 00:58:45,481
Oi!

674
00:58:46,065 --> 00:58:47,650
(BREATHING HEAVILY)

675
00:58:54,114 --> 00:58:55,366
Give it.

676
00:59:05,668 --> 00:59:07,086
(BREATHING HEAVILY)

677
00:59:08,837 --> 00:59:09,964
Give it.

678
00:59:27,314 --> 00:59:28,983
Those men that hurt you,

679
00:59:32,278 --> 00:59:33,445
do they know you're here?

680
00:59:39,118 --> 00:59:40,119
Hmm.

681
00:59:46,959 --> 00:59:48,252
What's my name?

682
00:59:54,383 --> 00:59:55,467
Say my name.

683
00:59:57,678 --> 00:59:58,762
Randolph.

684
01:00:02,599 --> 01:00:03,851
(SIGHS)

685
01:00:04,601 --> 01:00:05,728
That's right.

686
01:00:11,108 --> 01:00:12,609
Who's Little John?

687
01:00:14,903 --> 01:00:18,282
That name is nonsense
and you should never speak it.

688
01:00:18,282 --> 01:00:20,909
And if you speak it,
a cat will come in the night

689
01:00:20,909 --> 01:00:22,119
and take your tongue.

690
01:00:22,119 --> 01:00:24,163
(BREATH TREMBLING)

691
01:00:29,501 --> 01:00:30,544
(SIGHS)

692
01:00:32,379 --> 01:00:33,547
It's all right.

693
01:00:36,592 --> 01:00:37,676
Go to sleep.

694
01:00:40,054 --> 01:00:42,097
(WIND WHISTLING)

695
01:00:45,225 --> 01:00:46,518
ROBIN: I'll watch the storm.

696
01:00:50,939 --> 01:00:52,858
All right, lay down.

697
01:00:56,862 --> 01:00:57,988
Close your eyes.

698
01:01:03,160 --> 01:01:04,286
You can sleep.

699
01:01:09,583 --> 01:01:10,709
Sleep.

700
01:01:49,289 --> 01:01:51,500
SISTER BRIGID: Thank you
for bringing her back.

701
01:01:53,877 --> 01:01:55,003
It were nothing.

702
01:02:00,467 --> 01:02:02,553
Do you know the story
of this Priory?

703
01:02:03,595 --> 01:02:04,721
ROBIN: No.

704
01:02:07,224 --> 01:02:09,143
Well, in ages before history,

705
01:02:09,143 --> 01:02:10,686
this was a holy site

706
01:02:10,686 --> 01:02:12,229
of the ancient Druids.

707
01:02:14,314 --> 01:02:16,108
They worshipped unknown gods.

708
01:02:17,901 --> 01:02:19,403
Worshipped in these woods.

709
01:02:20,904 --> 01:02:23,407
Then the Romans came long ago,
and they sensed

710
01:02:23,407 --> 01:02:24,783
the magic of this place,

711
01:02:24,783 --> 01:02:26,493
so they built their temples.

712
01:02:28,412 --> 01:02:30,164
And people upon people
sensed it,

713
01:02:30,164 --> 01:02:32,040
and they came here to worship.

714
01:02:33,625 --> 01:02:35,461
Countless gods,
tribe after tribe,

715
01:02:35,461 --> 01:02:38,255
who would not even know
how to speak to each other.

716
01:02:39,506 --> 01:02:41,717
Yet we were all
pulled to this place.

717
01:02:43,844 --> 01:02:46,346
ROBIN: Do you believe
there is magic in this island?

718
01:02:48,849 --> 01:02:50,559
I believe there is power
in it.

719
01:02:53,437 --> 01:02:55,647
I have seen
the power in stories.

720
01:02:58,275 --> 01:03:01,570
They can be used to make men
do terrible things.

721
01:03:02,738 --> 01:03:06,617
And then those terrible things
become stories.

722
01:03:07,951 --> 01:03:09,077
And stories...

723
01:03:13,415 --> 01:03:14,458
No.

724
01:03:15,209 --> 01:03:16,752
You cannot trust any of them.

725
01:03:17,586 --> 01:03:19,505
We cannot be
trusted with them.

726
01:03:21,757 --> 01:03:24,718
Knives cut bread
as well as they do flesh.

727
01:03:26,470 --> 01:03:27,930
It's a question of balance.

728
01:03:28,889 --> 01:03:30,682
ROBIN: Does balance
have a say?

729
01:03:33,185 --> 01:03:35,646
I think if balance
did not have some say,

730
01:03:36,730 --> 01:03:38,190
we would not be here.

731
01:03:40,400 --> 01:03:44,154
This world would not...
be here and...

732
01:03:45,656 --> 01:03:47,699
we would not be
sharing these words.

733
01:03:48,784 --> 01:03:50,202
And do you ever consider that?

734
01:03:52,454 --> 01:03:54,122
The incredible precision
of balance

735
01:03:54,122 --> 01:03:56,625
over such an expanse of time.

736
01:03:58,085 --> 01:04:02,548
The balance it takes to create
a word, any specific word.

737
01:04:02,548 --> 01:04:05,259
That can pass
between lips and...

738
01:04:07,177 --> 01:04:09,388
be understood by two minds.

739
01:04:16,895 --> 01:04:18,021
Randolph.

740
01:04:22,150 --> 01:04:23,277
Thank you.

741
01:05:04,860 --> 01:05:05,944
(ROBIN GRUNTS)

742
01:05:28,925 --> 01:05:30,844
-ROBIN: Mmm. Mmm.
-SISTER BRIGID: Mmm.

743
01:05:42,397 --> 01:05:43,523
Not hungry?

744
01:05:45,442 --> 01:05:46,693
No?

745
01:06:19,393 --> 01:06:20,936
THE LEPER: I'll be dying soon.

746
01:06:22,604 --> 01:06:23,772
ROBIN: How do you know?

747
01:06:26,149 --> 01:06:27,984
THE LEPER: I can
feel it inside.

748
01:06:30,612 --> 01:06:32,239
ROBIN: What does it feel like?

749
01:06:34,533 --> 01:06:35,992
THE LEPER: It feels right.

750
01:06:40,038 --> 01:06:42,124
(SEAGULL SQUAWKING)

751
01:06:47,629 --> 01:06:49,131
There's someone there.

752
01:06:52,217 --> 01:06:53,927
THE LEPER: The waters
have been busy.

753
01:06:57,389 --> 01:06:58,598
ROBIN: Start around the feet.

754
01:06:59,224 --> 01:07:00,267
Little cuts.

755
01:07:00,767 --> 01:07:01,893
Good. Other one.

756
01:07:04,104 --> 01:07:06,231
All right. Oh.

757
01:07:07,983 --> 01:07:09,109
(SOFTLY) Okay.

758
01:07:10,152 --> 01:07:11,737
Good.

759
01:07:11,737 --> 01:07:13,447
Right, now,
let's do down the front.

760
01:07:13,447 --> 01:07:15,115
Right down on the belly. Good.

761
01:07:16,241 --> 01:07:18,285
Right. Give me.

762
01:07:18,285 --> 01:07:20,245
Right. Watch this one.

763
01:07:20,245 --> 01:07:22,873
Just like... just like that.

764
01:07:22,873 --> 01:07:24,624
That's it.
You do the other one.

765
01:07:26,042 --> 01:07:28,378
That's it, that's it,
that's it.

766
01:07:28,378 --> 01:07:29,379
Pull.

767
01:07:32,591 --> 01:07:34,634
(INDISTINCT CHATTER)

768
01:07:40,474 --> 01:07:42,559
(OMINOUS MUSIC PLAYING)

769
01:07:47,731 --> 01:07:49,399
(SISTER BRIGID
SPEAKING INDISTINCTLY)

770
01:08:05,081 --> 01:08:07,125
SISTER BRIGID: You are a brave
young man.

771
01:08:08,251 --> 01:08:09,544
ARTHUR: Thank you, sister.

772
01:08:11,546 --> 01:08:12,881
Are you hungry, Arthur?

773
01:08:13,673 --> 01:08:16,718
(BREATHES DEEPLY)

774
01:08:16,718 --> 01:08:18,261
I am, sister.

775
01:08:18,261 --> 01:08:20,931
Well, Sarah will fix you up
a good supper tonight.

776
01:08:20,931 --> 01:08:23,266
Herring stew. Herring stew.

777
01:08:25,310 --> 01:08:28,230
-Do you like that?
-Yes.

778
01:08:28,230 --> 01:08:29,648
Where do you come from,
Arthur?

779
01:08:33,527 --> 01:08:35,153
My family farm
toward Keswick.

780
01:08:35,153 --> 01:08:37,364
-ROBIN: Toward Keswick?
-Yes, Sir.

781
01:08:37,364 --> 01:08:40,283
-Randolph
-Randolph. Toward Keswick.

782
01:08:40,283 --> 01:08:42,160
The Leper said
you seek your niece.

783
01:08:43,161 --> 01:08:44,621
-I do.
-You were set upon?

784
01:08:47,040 --> 01:08:48,583
-I was.
-On the road?

785
01:08:50,502 --> 01:08:51,878
Yes.

786
01:08:51,878 --> 01:08:53,463
-You are safe here.
-Which road?

787
01:08:55,507 --> 01:08:56,925
The road to Keswick, Sir.

788
01:08:58,510 --> 01:09:00,178
Thank the Lord you survived.

789
01:09:00,178 --> 01:09:01,638
I do thank the Lord.

790
01:09:01,638 --> 01:09:03,515
How about honeyed pears
after supper

791
01:09:03,515 --> 01:09:04,891
for being so brave?

792
01:09:07,978 --> 01:09:10,146
-Thank you, sister.
-Good.

793
01:09:23,660 --> 01:09:25,078
Little Margaret.

794
01:09:26,454 --> 01:09:27,956
That man, Arthur.

795
01:09:27,956 --> 01:09:30,750
Did you ever see him
before you came to the Priory?

796
01:09:34,004 --> 01:09:35,046
Margaret.

797
01:09:42,554 --> 01:09:46,057
The men...
The men who hurt your papa.

798
01:09:48,768 --> 01:09:50,437
Did you see their faces?

799
01:09:51,688 --> 01:09:53,189
Papa said to run.

800
01:09:54,566 --> 01:09:55,859
(LITTLE MARGARET SIGHS)

801
01:10:04,200 --> 01:10:05,452
Papa stopped praying.

802
01:10:07,412 --> 01:10:08,580
He was angry.

803
01:10:08,580 --> 01:10:10,040
(SIGHS, MUTTERS)

804
01:10:10,040 --> 01:10:11,416
He prayed in his mind.

805
01:10:14,836 --> 01:10:15,962
What's Papa's name?

806
01:10:18,465 --> 01:10:19,507
(ARROW THUDS)

807
01:10:20,300 --> 01:10:21,551
Your papa's your papa.

808
01:10:34,356 --> 01:10:35,482
Bring it here.

809
01:10:37,692 --> 01:10:38,777
(ROBIN GRUNTS SOFTLY)

810
01:10:45,116 --> 01:10:47,118
Oh, this is fine work.

811
01:10:51,289 --> 01:10:52,332
Oh.

812
01:10:53,083 --> 01:10:54,876
You see it favors
a bend here.

813
01:10:56,503 --> 01:11:00,173
Oh, aye. Now you have to
balance it so it bends evenly.

814
01:11:01,257 --> 01:11:04,970
So this part here needs
to be thinned. All right?

815
01:11:10,433 --> 01:11:11,810
Can you make for me?

816
01:11:14,729 --> 01:11:16,189
You want me
to make you a bow?

817
01:11:20,819 --> 01:11:21,945
All right.

818
01:11:23,279 --> 01:11:24,406
(CHUCKLES SOFTLY)

819
01:11:29,995 --> 01:11:31,538
SISTER BRIGID: Today,
I remembered a story

820
01:11:31,538 --> 01:11:33,790
by a poet named Lucretius.

821
01:11:33,790 --> 01:11:34,958
(FIRE CRACKLES)

822
01:11:35,875 --> 01:11:37,836
He wrote on
what are called atoms.

823
01:11:39,629 --> 01:11:41,673
Where if you pick apart
the trees

824
01:11:41,673 --> 01:11:43,341
and people and stones,

825
01:11:43,341 --> 01:11:46,928
these would be the smallest
invisible pieces

826
01:11:46,928 --> 01:11:50,348
that make up
everything in creation.

827
01:11:52,434 --> 01:11:56,771
And these atoms are sailing
through the void and...

828
01:11:59,649 --> 01:12:01,443
well, sometimes,
for no reason at all,

829
01:12:01,443 --> 01:12:02,777
they change their course.

830
01:12:05,864 --> 01:12:08,033
The least amount,
not even a hair.

831
01:12:12,912 --> 01:12:16,416
And if it weren't
for that small...

832
01:12:17,500 --> 01:12:21,588
unpredictable change
forcing the atoms to cross

833
01:12:21,588 --> 01:12:23,423
and weave with each other...

834
01:12:26,676 --> 01:12:29,429
nothing at all
under the heavens would exist.

835
01:12:31,264 --> 01:12:32,766
(FOOTSTEPS APPROACHING)

836
01:13:22,107 --> 01:13:23,399
(INCENSE SIZZLES)

837
01:13:25,777 --> 01:13:28,488
(BREATHES DEEPLY)

838
01:13:37,997 --> 01:13:39,666
(EXHALES)

839
01:13:40,750 --> 01:13:43,253
(BREATHING DEEPLY)

840
01:13:49,843 --> 01:13:51,928
(MOANING SOFTLY)

841
01:14:24,627 --> 01:14:27,255
(GASPING SOFTLY)

842
01:14:32,969 --> 01:14:35,096
(EXHALES SHARPLY)

843
01:14:35,722 --> 01:14:36,764
(MOANS AND GASPS)

844
01:14:52,947 --> 01:14:54,699
(EXHALES DEEPLY)

845
01:15:13,509 --> 01:15:15,553
ROBIN: Yes. Good.

846
01:15:15,553 --> 01:15:16,888
-(CHUCKLES)
-Harder.

847
01:15:17,597 --> 01:15:18,932
That's it. Good.

848
01:15:18,932 --> 01:15:22,101
-(LAUGHS)
-(CHUCKLES)

849
01:15:22,101 --> 01:15:24,479
That's good. Harder.

850
01:15:25,313 --> 01:15:26,856
Let me help you.

851
01:15:26,856 --> 01:15:28,233
(INDISTINCT CHATTER)

852
01:15:28,233 --> 01:15:29,317
ROBIN: Grab that one.

853
01:15:30,610 --> 01:15:32,695
Good. There we go!

854
01:15:33,154 --> 01:15:34,197
(CHUCKLES) Good.

855
01:15:34,197 --> 01:15:36,282
(CHILDREN CHATTERING)

856
01:15:37,617 --> 01:15:39,285
Which one do you think
for your bow?

857
01:15:40,245 --> 01:15:41,412
(MUMBLES) Let's hold and look.

858
01:15:42,330 --> 01:15:43,373
Okay.

859
01:15:44,249 --> 01:15:46,334
There we go.
They're both good.

860
01:15:46,334 --> 01:15:47,502
(LITTLE MARGARET
SIGHS CONTENTEDLY)

861
01:15:48,753 --> 01:15:50,129
(CHATTER CONTINUES)

862
01:15:57,595 --> 01:15:59,180
BOY: Margaret!

863
01:15:59,180 --> 01:16:00,932
Margaret! Come and play!

864
01:16:00,932 --> 01:16:03,101
(CHILDREN CHATTERING
INDISTINCTLY)

865
01:16:07,397 --> 01:16:09,983
(CHILDREN LAUGHING
AND CHATTERING)

866
01:16:22,328 --> 01:16:24,414
(OMINOUS MUSIC PLAYING)

867
01:16:28,960 --> 01:16:30,503
(CHATTER CONTINUES)

868
01:16:41,264 --> 01:16:42,348
ROBIN: Arthur.

869
01:16:43,683 --> 01:16:45,226
Do you know how to trap?

870
01:16:45,226 --> 01:16:47,061
-Yes.
-Good.

871
01:16:47,061 --> 01:16:48,980
I need a strong hand. Come.

872
01:17:02,243 --> 01:17:03,745
ROBIN:
Does your family have land?

873
01:17:05,580 --> 01:17:06,581
Yes.

874
01:17:08,499 --> 01:17:09,959
ROBIN: How many
of you are there?

875
01:17:12,086 --> 01:17:13,254
ARTHUR: I don't know.

876
01:17:16,466 --> 01:17:18,051
(GRUNTS, GROANS)

877
01:17:20,219 --> 01:17:21,471
-ARTHUR: Are you all right?
-(SIGHS)

878
01:17:23,097 --> 01:17:24,807
I am. I am.

879
01:17:24,807 --> 01:17:26,726
Just not as young
as I once were.

880
01:17:28,227 --> 01:17:29,437
(SIGHS)

881
01:17:29,437 --> 01:17:31,022
I could use a hand up is all.

882
01:17:33,191 --> 01:17:34,817
(GRUNTS, GROANS)

883
01:17:36,027 --> 01:17:37,070
Thank you.

884
01:17:38,696 --> 01:17:40,656
Oh aye, they delivered you
quite a blow.

885
01:17:42,784 --> 01:17:43,993
-Yes.
-Who did it?

886
01:17:46,788 --> 01:17:47,872
A man.

887
01:17:48,414 --> 01:17:49,499
Some men.

888
01:17:50,625 --> 01:17:52,585
-Bandits.
-A man, bandits?

889
01:17:53,628 --> 01:17:55,338
-Yes.
-Huh. May I look?

890
01:17:59,550 --> 01:18:00,635
(ROBIN INHALES SHARPLY)

891
01:18:01,427 --> 01:18:03,388
It's quite a blow.

892
01:18:03,388 --> 01:18:06,933
Oh, you're lucky you survived.
You're a lucky boy.

893
01:18:06,933 --> 01:18:07,892
Yes.

894
01:18:07,892 --> 01:18:10,269
You should be thankful
to be alive.

895
01:18:10,269 --> 01:18:12,563
-Yes.
-He used a quarterstaff?

896
01:18:18,903 --> 01:18:20,238
Hmm.

897
01:18:20,238 --> 01:18:22,031
Lucky it didn't
take your head off.

898
01:18:25,326 --> 01:18:26,828
(ROBIN SIGHS)

899
01:18:26,828 --> 01:18:28,538
BOY: (IN DISTANCE)
Randolph.

900
01:18:31,207 --> 01:18:32,542
Randolph.

901
01:18:35,503 --> 01:18:36,629
(SHOUTS) Moshe?

902
01:18:40,258 --> 01:18:42,760
The Prioress said to come.

903
01:18:43,553 --> 01:18:45,096
(BIRDS CHIRPING)

904
01:18:50,059 --> 01:18:52,145
(THE LEPER BREATHES WEAKLY)

905
01:19:19,422 --> 01:19:20,673
(WHEEZES)

906
01:19:24,427 --> 01:19:26,637
-Are you in great pain?
-(THE LEPER GROANS)

907
01:19:31,893 --> 01:19:33,060
(GROANS)

908
01:19:35,688 --> 01:19:37,690
Do you remember the trees?

909
01:19:40,276 --> 01:19:42,195
Pears, apples
and elderberries.

910
01:19:44,864 --> 01:19:45,990
Wonderful.

911
01:19:48,034 --> 01:19:49,202
(BREATHING HOARSELY)

912
01:19:52,705 --> 01:19:53,789
(GROANS)

913
01:19:56,584 --> 01:19:59,670
You know that you are
the most important person

914
01:19:59,670 --> 01:20:01,506
in Little Margaret's life.

915
01:20:02,173 --> 01:20:04,967
Ah, I'm not important.

916
01:20:05,635 --> 01:20:06,719
(THE LEPER GROANS)

917
01:20:12,558 --> 01:20:13,559
(GROANS)

918
01:20:17,647 --> 01:20:19,357
Think of all the people

919
01:20:19,357 --> 01:20:21,150
whose lives you've crossed.

920
01:20:22,568 --> 01:20:24,403
(THE LEPER GROANS)

921
01:20:24,403 --> 01:20:27,114
We were important somehow
to all of them.

922
01:20:34,664 --> 01:20:37,750
Do you know the story
of Guy of Gisborne

923
01:20:39,877 --> 01:20:41,420
and Robin Hood?

924
01:20:46,676 --> 01:20:47,843
Yes.

925
01:20:52,765 --> 01:20:55,017
They say you killed him.

926
01:20:55,935 --> 01:20:57,103
(THE LEPER GROANS)

927
01:20:57,103 --> 01:20:58,646
Did you really behead him?

928
01:20:58,646 --> 01:21:01,857
-You are confused, leper.
-Well, I'll not tell a soul.

929
01:21:04,610 --> 01:21:07,530
But don't lie to a man
on his deathbed.

930
01:21:10,157 --> 01:21:13,786
So, did you really behead him?

931
01:21:17,331 --> 01:21:19,625
There were no Guy of Gisborne.

932
01:21:19,625 --> 01:21:22,712
It were just one of the many
tales that passed along.

933
01:21:25,047 --> 01:21:26,674
But I beheaded other men.

934
01:21:29,427 --> 01:21:31,012
(BREATHES HEAVILY)

935
01:21:34,432 --> 01:21:36,309
You really don't remember?

936
01:21:37,518 --> 01:21:38,894
(THE LEPER SCOFFS SOFTLY)

937
01:21:38,894 --> 01:21:41,188
No. (SNORTS, GULPS)

938
01:21:41,188 --> 01:21:42,732
Think about that name.

939
01:21:47,445 --> 01:21:49,780
There were a Guy of Gisborne.

940
01:21:51,324 --> 01:21:52,408
(THE LEPER SIGHS)

941
01:21:53,618 --> 01:21:55,369
But you did not behead him.

942
01:21:58,247 --> 01:21:59,582
You cut off his ear.

943
01:22:03,711 --> 01:22:05,796
(SOMBER MUSIC PLAYING)

944
01:22:07,340 --> 01:22:12,470
You said, in another life,
you'd share a drink with him.

945
01:22:12,470 --> 01:22:13,721
(WHEEZES)

946
01:22:14,805 --> 01:22:16,098
Do you remember that?

947
01:22:28,235 --> 01:22:31,447
We seem to have
found another life.

948
01:22:35,076 --> 01:22:36,118
(SIGHS)

949
01:22:38,454 --> 01:22:41,332
THE LEPER: Now,
there is one cruelty

950
01:22:41,332 --> 01:22:43,959
I would ask you
to not bring about.

951
01:22:43,959 --> 01:22:46,337
(WHEEZES)

952
01:22:46,337 --> 01:22:48,464
(BREATHING WEAKLY) You will
guard the orchard

953
01:22:48,464 --> 01:22:50,633
until you too die.

954
01:22:53,719 --> 01:22:55,971
You will serve the Prioress...

955
01:22:57,640 --> 01:23:01,102
and the people of this Priory,
past and future.

956
01:23:05,940 --> 01:23:09,735
But at no point
in that time...

957
01:23:12,446 --> 01:23:16,659
in those
good and peaceful years,

958
01:23:19,745 --> 01:23:23,165
will you tell the Prioress
who you really are.

959
01:23:23,165 --> 01:23:24,291
(BREATHES HEAVILY)

960
01:23:24,291 --> 01:23:26,419
(SOMBER MUSIC
CONTINUES PLAYING)

961
01:23:29,588 --> 01:23:30,673
Why?

962
01:23:33,175 --> 01:23:34,260
Because...

963
01:23:36,178 --> 01:23:38,180
one of those nameless,

964
01:23:38,180 --> 01:23:39,432
faceless deaths

965
01:23:39,432 --> 01:23:41,392
in the shadows of your mind...

966
01:23:44,478 --> 01:23:46,063
were in another life...

967
01:23:49,024 --> 01:23:50,359
her beloved.

968
01:23:51,444 --> 01:23:53,612
(THE LEPER CONTINUES
BREATHING WEAKLY)

969
01:23:58,868 --> 01:23:59,994
Promise me.

970
01:24:05,291 --> 01:24:06,333
Robin...

971
01:24:08,252 --> 01:24:09,378
Promise me.

972
01:24:15,050 --> 01:24:17,136
(TENDER MUSIC PLAYING)

973
01:24:36,781 --> 01:24:44,246
WOMAN: ♪ Sumer is icumen in
Loude sing cuccu! ♪

974
01:24:44,246 --> 01:24:47,541
♪ Groweth seed
and bloweth meed ♪

975
01:24:47,541 --> 01:24:51,212
♪ And springth the wode now ♪

976
01:24:51,212 --> 01:24:54,799
♪ Sing cuccu ♪

977
01:24:54,799 --> 01:25:01,222
♪ Ewe bleteth after lamb
Loweth after calve cow ♪

978
01:25:01,222 --> 01:25:05,059
♪ Bulloc sterteth
bucke verteth ♪

979
01:25:05,059 --> 01:25:08,020
♪ Merye sing cuccu! ♪

980
01:25:08,020 --> 01:25:14,568
♪ Cuccu, cuccu
Wel singest thou cuccu! ♪

981
01:25:14,568 --> 01:25:16,612
♪ Ne swik thou never now! ♪

982
01:25:16,612 --> 01:25:18,781
-(SONG FADES)
-(FOOTSTEPS APPROACHING)

983
01:25:24,870 --> 01:25:26,956
(SENTIMENTAL MUSIC PLAYING)

984
01:25:37,049 --> 01:25:38,843
(FOOTSTEPS RECEDING)

985
01:25:41,345 --> 01:25:42,721
ROBIN: (WHISPERS)
Good-bye, Brigid.

986
01:25:52,815 --> 01:25:54,066
(FIRE CRACKLES)

987
01:25:56,318 --> 01:25:57,403
ROBIN: Arthur.

988
01:25:59,822 --> 01:26:00,948
Come.

989
01:26:01,991 --> 01:26:03,993
(BOAT CREAKING)

990
01:26:03,993 --> 01:26:05,828
There's a child
on the far shore.

991
01:26:07,538 --> 01:26:08,831
Perhaps it's your niece.

992
01:26:11,000 --> 01:26:13,085
The Leper said
you sought your lost niece.

993
01:26:14,795 --> 01:26:16,630
Yes.

994
01:26:16,630 --> 01:26:19,091
-Could it be her?
-We shall see.

995
01:26:21,260 --> 01:26:23,345
(DRAMATIC MUSIC PLAYING)

996
01:26:58,422 --> 01:27:00,299
I do not see signs of anyone.

997
01:27:01,759 --> 01:27:02,968
Ah, we should wait.

998
01:27:03,928 --> 01:27:05,262
Maybe they will return.

999
01:27:27,701 --> 01:27:28,827
What is your name?

1000
01:27:32,998 --> 01:27:34,124
Arthur.

1001
01:27:35,417 --> 01:27:37,586
Do not waste time lying to me.

1002
01:27:37,586 --> 01:27:38,754
What is your real name?

1003
01:27:40,631 --> 01:27:42,216
(BREATH TREMBLES)

1004
01:27:43,676 --> 01:27:44,802
Godwyn.

1005
01:27:48,055 --> 01:27:50,557
You are deciding, Godwyn,

1006
01:27:50,557 --> 01:27:52,643
whether to do
the deed yourself...

1007
01:27:53,936 --> 01:27:55,396
or to fetch your family.

1008
01:27:58,857 --> 01:27:59,942
What deed?

1009
01:27:59,942 --> 01:28:02,069
How many men
have you killed, Godwyn?

1010
01:28:03,529 --> 01:28:06,323
How many children
have you killed?

1011
01:28:06,323 --> 01:28:08,409
(BREATHING HEAVILY)

1012
01:28:11,620 --> 01:28:14,707
I have killed so many,
I could not give you a count.

1013
01:28:16,458 --> 01:28:18,502
For longer than
you've been alive.

1014
01:28:18,502 --> 01:28:21,338
I've been killing the kin of
those I killed a lifetime ago.

1015
01:28:21,338 --> 01:28:23,340
I do not remember the first,

1016
01:28:23,340 --> 01:28:25,634
who meant nothing to me
at the time, but...

1017
01:28:27,052 --> 01:28:29,054
their brothers
and their children,

1018
01:28:29,054 --> 01:28:31,557
their grandchildren,
they remember me.

1019
01:28:32,683 --> 01:28:34,560
So now I must kill them.

1020
01:28:35,686 --> 01:28:38,230
Again and again.

1021
01:28:38,230 --> 01:28:42,151
In different forms,
like shades. Again.

1022
01:28:43,527 --> 01:28:45,612
And again,
and it never ceases.

1023
01:28:49,241 --> 01:28:51,035
It's a curse, Godwyn.

1024
01:28:52,911 --> 01:28:54,496
And it's one
you bring on yourself,

1025
01:28:54,496 --> 01:28:57,249
even if done through duty.

1026
01:28:58,125 --> 01:29:00,210
It cares not about your duty.

1027
01:29:01,086 --> 01:29:04,006
This world cares
only about blood.

1028
01:29:05,758 --> 01:29:07,968
And blood is all
it will give in return.

1029
01:29:10,679 --> 01:29:13,557
But do you want
to kill a child, Godwyn?

1030
01:29:16,018 --> 01:29:17,519
Do you want to be responsible

1031
01:29:17,519 --> 01:29:19,938
for the killing
of a little girl?

1032
01:29:20,856 --> 01:29:21,940
(SNIFFLES)

1033
01:29:23,400 --> 01:29:24,485
(VOICE BREAKING) No.

1034
01:29:36,413 --> 01:29:37,915
Your father's dead?

1035
01:29:39,541 --> 01:29:40,709
And your uncles?

1036
01:29:40,709 --> 01:29:41,752
(SNIFFLES)

1037
01:29:43,879 --> 01:29:45,380
I am the eldest left.

1038
01:29:46,882 --> 01:29:47,925
(SIGHS)

1039
01:29:50,094 --> 01:29:51,428
Who else yet lives, Godwyn?

1040
01:29:54,056 --> 01:29:55,182
My mother.

1041
01:29:56,892 --> 01:29:57,976
My aunts.

1042
01:29:59,603 --> 01:30:01,021
My sisters.

1043
01:30:01,021 --> 01:30:03,023
You have a home, Godwyn?

1044
01:30:05,359 --> 01:30:07,152
-Yes.
-Go home.

1045
01:30:08,362 --> 01:30:11,115
Tell your family
all blood debts are paid.

1046
01:30:11,865 --> 01:30:13,450
They'll all be paid someday.

1047
01:30:15,911 --> 01:30:17,162
Sleep in your own bed.

1048
01:30:18,413 --> 01:30:20,541
Hold dear the life

1049
01:30:20,541 --> 01:30:22,626
you will yet share
with your kin.

1050
01:30:24,294 --> 01:30:25,963
And if you return here,

1051
01:30:27,214 --> 01:30:28,757
you will have wasted
this chance

1052
01:30:28,757 --> 01:30:30,509
at life that I give you now.

1053
01:30:32,386 --> 01:30:34,179
And I will cut your throat

1054
01:30:34,179 --> 01:30:36,056
and never think of you again.

1055
01:30:42,813 --> 01:30:43,939
Go now.

1056
01:30:46,483 --> 01:30:47,693
And do not look back.

1057
01:31:01,498 --> 01:31:03,250
(SIGHS HEAVILY)

1058
01:31:21,476 --> 01:31:23,520
(BIRDS CHIRPING)

1059
01:31:42,372 --> 01:31:44,750
I thought
you had departed. (SOBS)

1060
01:31:44,750 --> 01:31:46,627
(BREATHES HEAVILY)

1061
01:31:54,384 --> 01:31:55,886
I am Robin the Outlaw.

1062
01:31:59,556 --> 01:32:03,310
You... You need to know
that I'm a monster.

1063
01:32:05,646 --> 01:32:07,064
I'm not one Randolph.

1064
01:32:09,858 --> 01:32:13,070
I am the outlaw, Robin Hood.

1065
01:32:14,947 --> 01:32:17,032
(DRAMATIC MUSIC PLAYING)

1066
01:32:23,372 --> 01:32:25,415
(SOBBING BREATHS)

1067
01:32:27,709 --> 01:32:29,753
(MUMBLES) I'm sorry...

1068
01:32:33,882 --> 01:32:35,926
(BREATHING HEAVILY)

1069
01:32:44,768 --> 01:32:45,852
(GRUNTS)

1070
01:32:56,571 --> 01:32:57,990
(FIRE CRACKLES)

1071
01:32:57,990 --> 01:33:00,158
(DRAMATIC MUSIC
CONTINUES PLAYING)

1072
01:33:12,921 --> 01:33:14,006
(WILD BOAR GRUNTS)

1073
01:33:15,716 --> 01:33:17,801
(BOWSTRING STRETCHING)

1074
01:33:32,232 --> 01:33:33,275
(ARROW THUDS)

1075
01:33:33,275 --> 01:33:35,360
(WILD BOAR'S BODY DRAGGING)

1076
01:33:52,461 --> 01:33:53,462
(GROANS)

1077
01:33:58,633 --> 01:34:00,761
(DRAMATIC MUSIC
CONTINUES PLAYING)

1078
01:34:07,309 --> 01:34:08,477
(MUSIC FADES)

1079
01:34:10,020 --> 01:34:12,022
(PANTING)

1080
01:34:30,165 --> 01:34:31,625
Tell me I should leave.

1081
01:34:34,336 --> 01:34:35,462
I will go now.

1082
01:34:43,595 --> 01:34:44,763
Get in the bed.

1083
01:35:04,825 --> 01:35:06,618
-Brigid...
-Lie down.

1084
01:35:33,353 --> 01:35:34,396
(BOWL CLATTERS)

1085
01:35:46,992 --> 01:35:48,910
(ROBIN SIGHS)

1086
01:35:52,205 --> 01:35:53,832
(BREATHING DEEPLY)

1087
01:36:01,673 --> 01:36:03,758
(BLOOD CONTINUES DRIPPING)

1088
01:36:17,230 --> 01:36:19,316
(TRAGIC MUSIC PLAYING)

1089
01:36:31,161 --> 01:36:33,205
(BREATHING SLOWS)

1090
01:37:04,945 --> 01:37:05,987
(SIGHS SOFTLY)

1091
01:37:09,616 --> 01:37:10,825
(SIGHS)

1092
01:37:14,120 --> 01:37:16,289
(TRAGIC MUSIC
CONTINUES PLAYING)

1093
01:37:21,253 --> 01:37:22,337
(DOOR CLOSES)

1094
01:37:35,892 --> 01:37:37,435
(LAUGHS)

1095
01:37:37,435 --> 01:37:39,521
(DISTURBING MUSIC PLAYING)

1096
01:37:41,773 --> 01:37:43,858
(INDISTINCT CHATTERING)

1097
01:38:32,324 --> 01:38:34,492
You burned my husband alive.

1098
01:38:39,956 --> 01:38:41,583
I dream that
he was already dead,

1099
01:38:41,583 --> 01:38:44,169
but why else did you bar
the door? (SOBS)

1100
01:38:48,757 --> 01:38:50,216
It felt like a lifetime

1101
01:38:50,216 --> 01:38:52,802
searching the ashes
of our home.

1102
01:38:56,097 --> 01:38:57,849
And he looked so small.

1103
01:38:59,851 --> 01:39:03,647
Curled up in the corner
with so much of him gone.

1104
01:39:08,360 --> 01:39:09,778
But as much as I dream,

1105
01:39:09,778 --> 01:39:11,196
I know he was alive.

1106
01:39:15,992 --> 01:39:17,827
Because, until the very end,

1107
01:39:17,827 --> 01:39:19,412
he had wrapped himself

1108
01:39:19,412 --> 01:39:21,247
around our children.

1109
01:39:28,713 --> 01:39:30,382
(EXHALES)

1110
01:39:31,299 --> 01:39:33,385
(TRAGIC MUSIC PLAYING)

1111
01:39:54,614 --> 01:39:56,700
(FOOTSTEPS APPROACHING)

1112
01:39:59,327 --> 01:40:00,745
(DOOR CLOSES)

1113
01:40:02,664 --> 01:40:03,998
(KNOCK ON DOOR)

1114
01:40:04,958 --> 01:40:06,376
(DOOR RATTLES)

1115
01:40:08,920 --> 01:40:10,839
-LITTLE MARGARET: Randolph...
-(GASPS SOFTLY)

1116
01:40:12,882 --> 01:40:14,008
...it's me.

1117
01:40:16,094 --> 01:40:18,221
(BREATHING HEAVILY)

1118
01:40:18,221 --> 01:40:20,557
(FOOTSTEPS RECEDING)

1119
01:40:20,557 --> 01:40:22,684
(SOBBING SOFTLY)

1120
01:40:32,402 --> 01:40:34,487
(SISTER BRIGID SOBBING)

1121
01:40:56,593 --> 01:40:58,803
(BREATHES DEEPLY)

1122
01:41:08,229 --> 01:41:10,356
ROBIN: People speak
of Robin Hood.

1123
01:41:12,317 --> 01:41:13,359
(ROBIN SIGHS)

1124
01:41:15,445 --> 01:41:17,030
Tell his stories.

1125
01:41:23,411 --> 01:41:24,913
They're all lies.

1126
01:41:28,792 --> 01:41:31,544
Lies that I told long ago.

1127
01:41:33,296 --> 01:41:34,881
So that fools would...

1128
01:41:36,466 --> 01:41:38,384
follow me into the dark.

1129
01:41:40,261 --> 01:41:42,347
(BREATHING WEAKLY)

1130
01:41:47,936 --> 01:41:50,772
EDWARD: Robin. (ECHOING)

1131
01:41:55,652 --> 01:41:57,445
What have they done to you?

1132
01:42:03,451 --> 01:42:05,161
EDWARD: Don't be afraid.

1133
01:42:07,247 --> 01:42:09,999
I will rescue you
from this place.

1134
01:42:12,001 --> 01:42:14,212
I'll rescue Little Margaret.

1135
01:42:15,922 --> 01:42:19,092
And we're gonna start
a new life together.

1136
01:42:19,717 --> 01:42:20,802
Edward...

1137
01:42:25,181 --> 01:42:27,267
tell me about your wife.

1138
01:42:31,271 --> 01:42:33,356
(ROBIN BREATHING WEAKLY)

1139
01:42:37,151 --> 01:42:38,236
My wife.

1140
01:42:41,114 --> 01:42:42,240
ROBIN: Margaret.

1141
01:42:46,536 --> 01:42:47,662
(SOFTLY) Margaret.

1142
01:42:49,998 --> 01:42:52,000
My wife, Margaret.

1143
01:42:54,294 --> 01:42:55,837
Paint a portrait.

1144
01:42:58,423 --> 01:42:59,799
EDWARD: Paint a portrait?

1145
01:43:04,220 --> 01:43:05,555
She has hair...

1146
01:43:07,849 --> 01:43:09,058
red like...

1147
01:43:10,685 --> 01:43:12,645
the setting sun.

1148
01:43:15,565 --> 01:43:18,735
Like the setting summer sun.

1149
01:43:25,992 --> 01:43:27,118
Red like...

1150
01:43:27,869 --> 01:43:30,163
the setting summer...

1151
01:43:31,247 --> 01:43:32,373
sun.

1152
01:43:35,001 --> 01:43:36,169
(GASPS)

1153
01:43:36,169 --> 01:43:38,087
(INDISTINCT VOICES WHISPERING)

1154
01:43:50,558 --> 01:43:51,643
Robin...

1155
01:43:57,815 --> 01:44:00,109
Have you found
your right death?

1156
01:44:05,615 --> 01:44:07,533
(BIRDS CHIRPING)

1157
01:44:33,351 --> 01:44:35,478
(LIMPING FOOTSTEPS APPROACH)

1158
01:44:45,613 --> 01:44:46,614
(GRUNTS)

1159
01:44:47,573 --> 01:44:49,617
(PANTING)

1160
01:45:00,878 --> 01:45:02,839
(SOFTLY)
I'm ready to finish it.

1161
01:45:10,013 --> 01:45:11,764
I owe this life to you.

1162
01:45:19,605 --> 01:45:21,107
(WHISPERING) It is monstrous.

1163
01:45:25,611 --> 01:45:27,488
But I owe this life to you.

1164
01:45:28,281 --> 01:45:29,866
You owe me nothing.

1165
01:45:35,204 --> 01:45:37,498
I have never taken
a life before.

1166
01:45:39,083 --> 01:45:40,126
Ah.

1167
01:45:41,878 --> 01:45:44,380
It can be
as if pruning a flower.

1168
01:45:46,424 --> 01:45:48,134
Well, it should not be.

1169
01:45:48,134 --> 01:45:50,386
Aye, it should not.

1170
01:45:52,096 --> 01:45:53,306
But it can.

1171
01:45:59,145 --> 01:46:01,564
I help those
who come to this island.

1172
01:46:04,525 --> 01:46:05,651
I heal them.

1173
01:46:09,739 --> 01:46:10,865
Please.

1174
01:46:16,412 --> 01:46:17,747
Heal me.

1175
01:46:22,585 --> 01:46:24,670
(SERENE MUSIC PLAYING)

1176
01:47:13,511 --> 01:47:15,054
Little Margaret...

1177
01:47:20,852 --> 01:47:23,146
your father's name were John.

1178
01:47:32,572 --> 01:47:34,615
I knew him for many years.

1179
01:47:35,992 --> 01:47:38,035
Before he were your papa.

1180
01:47:40,621 --> 01:47:41,747
(GRUNTS WEAKLY)

1181
01:47:44,000 --> 01:47:45,209
I met him...

1182
01:47:46,836 --> 01:47:48,796
crossing a bridge one day...

1183
01:47:50,464 --> 01:47:52,633
over a rushing river.

1184
01:47:55,219 --> 01:47:56,596
He was stubborn.

1185
01:47:59,849 --> 01:48:02,101
He would not yield.

1186
01:48:02,101 --> 01:48:05,313
Oh, not to any man.

1187
01:48:06,856 --> 01:48:08,232
Not even to me.

1188
01:48:11,694 --> 01:48:13,112
So we wrestled.

1189
01:48:13,988 --> 01:48:15,281
On that bridge.

1190
01:48:17,408 --> 01:48:20,578
Striking. Back and forth.

1191
01:48:22,663 --> 01:48:25,166
I whacked him
on the side of the head.

1192
01:48:27,710 --> 01:48:29,837
He struck me in the stomach.

1193
01:48:31,255 --> 01:48:33,549
And I wobbled on that bridge.

1194
01:48:35,468 --> 01:48:36,552
Splash!

1195
01:48:37,929 --> 01:48:40,139
-He knocked me on me bum.
-(CHUCKLES)

1196
01:48:44,101 --> 01:48:46,103
Once I dragged myself out,

1197
01:48:47,188 --> 01:48:48,773
drenched to the bone,

1198
01:48:50,650 --> 01:48:51,776
we embraced.

1199
01:48:55,446 --> 01:48:57,073
We called your papa...

1200
01:48:58,074 --> 01:48:59,200
Little John.

1201
01:49:00,785 --> 01:49:02,912
And we lived for many years...

1202
01:49:06,249 --> 01:49:09,710
as outlaws in the greenwood.

1203
01:49:12,797 --> 01:49:14,423
Oh, we had feasts.

1204
01:49:15,883 --> 01:49:16,884
We...

1205
01:49:17,551 --> 01:49:18,928
We gave silver...

1206
01:49:20,012 --> 01:49:21,138
to the meek.

1207
01:49:25,810 --> 01:49:27,311
(SOFTLY) No...

1208
01:49:38,406 --> 01:49:40,616
But we stole that silver.

1209
01:49:46,497 --> 01:49:47,623
We stole it...

1210
01:49:49,333 --> 01:49:52,586
from, from bad people.

1211
01:49:57,466 --> 01:49:58,634
(SOFTLY) You understand?

1212
01:50:01,304 --> 01:50:02,430
Bad people.

1213
01:50:12,189 --> 01:50:14,233
(BREATHING WEAKLY)

1214
01:50:17,862 --> 01:50:18,988
Your papa...

1215
01:50:20,489 --> 01:50:22,283
would want me to tell you...

1216
01:50:28,372 --> 01:50:31,000
because he loved you
so dearly.

1217
01:50:34,795 --> 01:50:37,256
You must never tell a soul.

1218
01:50:38,758 --> 01:50:41,135
Not even the children
you play with.

1219
01:50:42,762 --> 01:50:46,515
You must never tell them
your papa's name.

1220
01:50:50,686 --> 01:50:52,688
There are
bad people out there.

1221
01:50:55,566 --> 01:50:59,278
And they are angry
with your papa.

1222
01:51:00,780 --> 01:51:01,822
(ROBIN SIGHS)

1223
01:51:03,574 --> 01:51:05,785
(SOFTLY)
And so they're angry with you.

1224
01:51:05,785 --> 01:51:07,036
(SOBBING QUIETLY)

1225
01:51:10,790 --> 01:51:13,125
And can we keep that secret?

1226
01:51:15,378 --> 01:51:18,005
I can keep that secret,
Little Margaret.

1227
01:51:20,633 --> 01:51:21,717
Can you?

1228
01:51:26,389 --> 01:51:27,848
ROBIN: Good.

1229
01:51:29,934 --> 01:51:31,435
Now come beside me.

1230
01:51:34,730 --> 01:51:35,773
(SNIFFLES)

1231
01:51:41,612 --> 01:51:42,738
Take this.

1232
01:51:44,782 --> 01:51:46,075
Why were he "Little"?

1233
01:51:47,326 --> 01:51:48,411
What?

1234
01:51:49,453 --> 01:51:51,664
Why were my papa
called "Little"?

1235
01:51:51,664 --> 01:51:53,791
(ROBIN BREATHING SHALLOW)

1236
01:51:57,336 --> 01:51:59,755
Because he were little
when I found him.

1237
01:52:01,715 --> 01:52:02,800
Like you.

1238
01:52:02,800 --> 01:52:04,885
(TENDER MUSIC PLAYING)

1239
01:52:10,975 --> 01:52:12,101
String it.

1240
01:52:35,374 --> 01:52:38,544
Bend it with your leg
as you saw me do.

1241
01:52:47,094 --> 01:52:48,345
(BOW CREAKS)

1242
01:52:55,269 --> 01:52:56,395
(SOFTLY) Good.

1243
01:52:57,938 --> 01:53:01,734
Now, go to the window.

1244
01:53:10,201 --> 01:53:11,911
(BREATHING HEAVILY)

1245
01:53:18,834 --> 01:53:20,920
(TENDER MUSIC CONTINUES)

1246
01:53:22,421 --> 01:53:23,506
Now,

1247
01:53:25,549 --> 01:53:26,675
nock an arrow.

1248
01:53:35,476 --> 01:53:36,769
Find a point

1249
01:53:37,895 --> 01:53:40,231
where the waters meet the sky.

1250
01:53:45,402 --> 01:53:47,613
Pull that arrow to your mouth.

1251
01:53:51,742 --> 01:53:52,868
And hold.

1252
01:53:54,787 --> 01:53:56,830
I will tell you when to loose.

1253
01:54:00,042 --> 01:54:01,335
And when I do...

1254
01:54:02,461 --> 01:54:03,921
you're not letting go.

1255
01:54:06,882 --> 01:54:09,218
You're simply
not holding on anymore.

1256
01:54:14,181 --> 01:54:15,224
Now.

1257
01:54:16,850 --> 01:54:18,310
Watch that target.

1258
01:54:21,564 --> 01:54:23,857
Your body
will always be moving.

1259
01:54:27,027 --> 01:54:31,574
Feel when that arrow
will fly true.

1260
01:54:34,868 --> 01:54:36,996
You're gonna find a dance.

1261
01:54:39,290 --> 01:54:41,834
Hold until I tell you
to loose.

1262
01:54:45,421 --> 01:54:47,006
You are strong.

1263
01:54:48,173 --> 01:54:49,717
Feel...

1264
01:54:51,427 --> 01:54:52,761
that sway,

1265
01:54:56,307 --> 01:54:57,516
that dance.

1266
01:55:03,355 --> 01:55:04,732
(MUSIC FADES)

1267
01:55:04,732 --> 01:55:06,275
Now loose.

1268
01:55:06,275 --> 01:55:09,403
-(ARROW FIRES)
-(WAVES CRASHING)

1269
01:55:19,580 --> 01:55:21,665
(TENDER MUSIC RESUMES)

1270
01:56:57,469 --> 01:56:59,555
(SOLEMN MUSIC PLAYING)

1271
02:01:47,009 --> 02:01:48,051
(MUSIC FADES)

